Barack Obama will receive the Nobel Peace Prize as the fourth U.S. president
U.S. President Barack Obama will arrive in Oslo, Norway, for 26 hours to receive the Nobel Peace Prize as the fourth U.S. president in history.
Among other things, he receives a diploma, a medal and a cheque of $1.4 million for his exceptional efforts to strengthen world diplomacy and cooperation between nations.
The head of the White House will fly to the Norwegian metropolis in the morning with his wife Michelle and be busy.
He will first visit the Nobel Institute, where he will meet for the first time with five members of the committee selected by him in October from 172 people and 33 organizations.
The presidential couple then have a scheduled meeting with Norwegian King Harald V and Queen Sonja.
In the afternoon, the visit culminates in a ceremony at which Obama will accept the prestigious award.
He'll get it as the fourth U.S. president, but only the third to win the prize right in office.
The White House has already announced that Obama will talk about the Afghan war when he accepts the award.
The president does not want to avoid this issue because he realizes that he accepts the prize as a president who is currently at war in two countries.
A few days ago, he increased his efforts by sending more troops to Afghanistan, which he will not forget to highlight any of his critics.
During the ceremony, Obama will also receive a gold medal, a diploma and a cheque for ten million Swedish crowns (about 24 million Czech crowns).
He intends to put them on charitables, but he hasn't decided on a particular charity yet.
A busy schedule will take the president to a banquet, where, in addition to the Norwegian king and queens, there will also be a prime minister and 250 other invited guests.
From the beginning, Obama was rather restrained about the price.
He stated, for example, that he felt that he did not fully deserve it.
He also reiterated several times that it was not just a prize for him, but for all those who strive for the same values as him.
Immediately after the announcement, he said it was a "call for action" for him.
Conservatior accused of extortion
Litoměřice police accused the chairman of the Civic Society of Conservatiors Litoměřice of extortion.
In several cases over the past year, he appealed for some construction proceedings, for the withdrawal of the appeal demanded money from investors for his own benefit, police spokeswoman Alena Romová said.
Lubomír Studnička is the chairman of the Litoměřice Society of Conservators.
He is now in custody and faces up to three years in prison.
Sorting containers don't smell like Brno very much
In Prague, ordinary people can sort packaging from beverages, in South Moravian villages you will meet sorting containers at every step.
And in Brno?
There to reserve a few minutes before you find suitable garbage cans.
I don't want to say there's no waste at all.
But it seems to me that the fact that we are the second largest city in the Czech Republic are not sufficiently interested in this problem.
Brno is in the rainforest league of waste sorting not only because, as citizens, we can still throw paper, glass and plastics into sorting containers here, but above all by the number of garbage cans for such waste.
Have you ever tried, for example, throwing a PET bottle into the collection basket?
It's a ready-made kumst, it just takes time and strong nerves.
The only place I've found so far is on the Moravian Square opposite Potrefené Huse.
Don't let Mr. Onderka and my colleagues get mad at me, but I don't think it's a hell of a thing.
Not to mention, in other parts there is no honey sorting.
Many of my friends prefer not to sort the trash.
Out of laze.
It's far from the garbage cans.
I don't blame them.
After the party, I offered to throw away a few glass and plastic bottles.
But I didn't see any colored garbage cans on Kounicova Street.
Luckily, on the way to Shalina, I came to the right place.
However, the rubbish was overflowing from it.
After all, it is still the center of Brno, which is probably the most represented by the city, right?!
But ecology and some aesthetics probably don't care much about the Brno socialists.
They would rather buy a hockey extraleague for the city, which will embarrass us all over the country, rather than buy a few extra containers and offer to sort out other raw materials for cleaner Brno.
I am glamed that I was able to confirm my presumption after a moment of browsing on the Internet with official statistics.
According to EKO-KOM, Brno is really the worst in the whole South Moravian Region.
"Paroubek's" budget took money for both pensions and sick leave
After the left, despite the government's opinion, has added to farmers or officials, there may not be money in the budget for pensions, sick leave or building savings.
But also on interest on national debt or international litigation.
It will cause problems for those who will govern in the second half of next year, says Finance Minister Eduard Janota.
When social democrats and communists promoted the increase to some groups, they relied that on sensitive expenses, pensions or sick leave, the government additionally. "It's just a postponement of problems for the future, laying mines," Janota says.
Borrowed money pays more and more interest and sometimes has to come back.
There's not much to choose from where to get it, the state doesn't have.
If it does not reduce social benefits or the costs of running the state, it must raise taxes.
To all to whom the left has now added: firefighters, teachers, farmers.
And later their children.
People don't have to worry about pensions or sick leave, from whose account 1.8 billion have disappeared, they'll get them.
However, at the cost of becoming more indebted – next year there will not be only 163 billion in the coffers, but more.
In spite of the efforts of Minister Janota, the debt will grow more and faster, along with the cost of interest.
The state will need the money to reimburse it, and it will no longer be enough to slightly raise VAT or reduce maternity, as the government has done in its anti-deficiency package.
Due to the transfer of money in favor of several groups of voters, five billion will also be missing next year for the completion of highways and railways.
These are mainly already competed and unfolded sections, which cannot be redesigned and possibly cheaper.
Preserve, which is also not free.
It means the delay or cessation of thirty large buildings, the drawing of European money is also at risk, warns Karel Hanzelka, a spokesman for the Ministry of Transport.
The ČSSD has suggested that it will solve the loss of money in Solomonic, reaching into the dividends of ČEZ, which the state mostly owns, and from them will add the transport fund.
The problem is that the amount of the dividend is never certain, and it is certainly not a bottomless source.
Last year, 18 billion came from CEZ to the budget.
Every year, dividends are liquidated from environmental damages, supplemented by a pension account or served as a reserve for the upcoming pension reform.
In addition, according to EU rules, the actual budget deficit is increased to 5.7% of GDP.
However, this does not end the carnival of transfers.
Nearly three billion have been cut in the budget for various compensations to victims of communism and crimes, or expenses for litigation, even for building savings.
If the state can do without that money, it's not certain.
It's a lottery.
If we lose an international dispute, we'll have to pay for it anyway, says Janota.
The minister will then have to save money inside the resort or, in the worst case, touch the government's budget reserve.
One hundred millions are also missing from the two-billion-dollar Treasury's two-billion-dollar project, which is supposed to allow online to monitor what individual authorities are spending.
The first stage will start in January.
If the finance minister did not find the money elsewhere, the project would have to stop, sanctions will come, Janota warns.
Your next smartphone will handle two operating systems
In the future, Americans are counting on a mobile phone, on which the user will switch between different operating systems at the touch of a button.
Revealed plans sound promising.
Just press one key and you can transfer from Windows Mobile to Android in a few seconds.
This is exactly the option aimed at the American company VMware, which focuses mainly on the development of virtualization software for computers.
We will allow you to run two user profiles on your phone at the same time.
You can switch between them, you can divide them into home and work.
Both will work at the same time, Srinivas Krishnamurti of VMware suggested in an interview with Computerworld magazine.
It was presented in November last year and was presented for the first time a few days ago.
It will be on the market in 2012.
The virtualization of smartphones is no fiction.
VMware has already successfully demonstrated to reporters a test sample of a smartphone with two operating systems.
It was a modified Nokia N800 with 128 MB RAM, on which the aforementioned Windows Mobile and Android systems also ran side by side.
The development of new technology for mobile phones is in full swing.
Now VMware is working with European and U.S
The lack of snow in the mountains troubles hoteliers
Bare slopes don't just worry Krkonoše lifters.
The missing snow discourages people even from ordering ski stays in hotels and guesthouses.
That’s why you can still get free rooms in the Giant Mountains for all winter dates, including Christmas and New Year’s Eve.
We have great traffic to our website.
People are going through offers, finding out prices, but so far they're afraid to make binding orders.
And if they call us, they immediately ask if we think this Christmas will be in the snow or on mud," Martin Jandura, the operator of the information portal Spindl.Info, said.
Anyone who wants a New Year's Eve stay in Špindlerův Mlýn exactly according to your wishes should no longer hesitate with the order.
If you prefer to save money and the quality of accommodation is in second place, you can still try to wait a few days.
So far, Spindleruv hoteliers are offering only weekly stays on New Year's Eve.
I think they'll try to turn them in for a little while, but then they'll allow and they'll go for a shorter time.
And then the vacancies will decrease quickly," Jandura estimated.
Vacant beds in all winter dates are still offered by Vrchlaby travel agency Ingtours.
Now we're most interested in a Christmas week that's not sold out yet.
About half of the seats in our offer remain vacant.
That's why we've already prepared some cheaper last-minute Christmas stays," said Petr Schiefert, director of Ingtours.
New Year's Eve in the Giant Mountains can also be offered by the Vrchlaby travel agency, but there are fewer vacancies.
The first fortnight in February is also good for sales, the remaining winter dates are sold on average.
This year, the interest in winter stays is negatively affected by the fact that there is still no snow in the
People are waiting to see if they attack.
No one wants to spend the end of the year in the mountains without snow.
If the slopes are snowy during the week, Christmas will also be sold out," Schiefert said.
On the contrary, it is a little better than last year to occupy the rooms of the Horizont hotel in Pec pod Sněžkou.
We're about five percent better.
We have the last few places on New Year's Eve and Christmas.
There is a lot of interest in the Polish holidays in January and February is also selling well," informed the director of the best Pecký hotel, Karel Rada.
Eighty percent of New Year's Eve stays are also occupied by the Omnia hotel near the central parking lot in Janské Lázně.
On the contrary, for Christmas, half of the beds remain vacant in the newestest Janskolazen hotel.
We were pleasantly surprised this year by January, which we are already occupied by about sixty percent at the moment.
A little weaker so far is February, but as soon as snow falls, interest in stays in the Giant Mountains will definitely rise further, said the director of the hotel Omnia Erik Sporysch.
However, for a larger amount of snow, Krkonoše hoteliers have yet to get a taste.
It's supposed to cool down on Thursday, if there's going to be any precipitation, there should be snow in the mountains.
But then warming is to come again, informed Jiří Jakubský from the Hradec Králové department of the Czech Hydrometeorological Institute.
U.S.: Repetition is the mother of wisdom
It's comical with what schoolboy's aspiration Barack Obama, the president with the reputation of being the smartest, repeats the strategy of his predecessor, which he himself thought to be the dumbest.
When he finally sweated out his long-awaited Afghan doctrine, it turned out that he had copied it through a copier from Bush's Iraqi script three years ago.
Not only does he use the phrase "as in Iraq" several times in the text itself, but he did not hesitate to even use the name of Bush's statement in January 2007 as the title of his own declaration: "A New Way Forward."
Three years ago, he criticized Bush's idea of sending fresh military reinforcements to Iraq plagued by civil war as an "irresponsible and, in its consequences, catastrophic decision."
However, subsequent months have shown that Bush's strategy, based on the recommendations of field commanders, was the only possible and highly-successful one so muct that there is no talk about Iraq at all today in intelligence sessions.
It didn't improve Bush's reputation, but it objectively left his successor a much more grateful starting situation than might have been expected.
In Afghanistan, Barack Obama hopes a miracle will be repeated.
He was again asked to increase the military contingent by field commanders, in particular the commander-in-chief of the Afghan operation, General Stanley McCristal, who, despite subordinative habits, harshly criticized the hesitant shooters in the White House.
He asked for 40 thousand men so that he could break the situation for the better.
The president hesitated for three months, but he didn't fall for anything better, so at least he cheated the general by 10 thousand soldiers.
He was able to wrinkle another 5 thousand from Europe, even though he originally counted on at least 10.
The most problematic about Obama’s entire strategy seems to be his intention to start withdrawing troops from Afghanistan as early as 18 months and ending the process within three years. The entire Obama doctrine raises more questions that provides answers.
Many doubt that a half-hearted increase will give the same fruit as the procedure in Iraq.
Despite the fratricidal wars of recent years, Iraqi society is relatively well-ordered, its leaders honor the deal.
The Afghan situation is characterized by the opposite symptom - no one can be relied upon and no agreements are being adhered to.
Under these conditions, the Afghan security forces, accustomed to changing the master every day according to how convenient they are.
Political institutions, even if they will be formally established, will remain only a potemkin village behind which patriarchal tribal relations will continue.
Balancing the interests of different ethnic groups is extremely complex, given the lack of clarity of circumstances that further complicate Pakistani and Iranian influences.
Even the construction of an "accompanying state," which is the minimalist goal of the whole operation, does not seem very real.
Obama's time-time strategy strikes with his voluntarism.
Over the past eight years, the situation in Afghanistan has only deteriorated and is now on the brink of an explosion.
To believe that soldiers sent into the turmoil for the purpose of an "early return home" will cause a miracle done here, only a truly romantic soul, untouched by reality.
From a political point of view, determining the date of withdrawal of troops is pure irresponsibility, because the Taliban will see this as an indirect granting of U.S. defeat.
NATO's last summit has showned that Europe's willingness to take part in combat operations in Afghanistan is minus.
European politicians do not and do not want to explain to their constituents how the security of Germany or Italy relates to a war in the foothills of the Hindus.
Another factor able to radically intervene in the situation is the development of events around Iran.
If the situation continues to deteriorate and intensified sanctions don't impress Iranians, there will be an overaging question before the United States about not attempting to solve the problem by military force.
It will have to be answered at a time, according to Obama, that America will proceed with a victorious withdrawal from Afghanistan.
However, any such solution will also mean a dramatic turnaround in the Afghan situation.
The only patch for Barack Obama is that there are three years left until the presidential election, and therefore there is still plenty of time left for him to come up with another strategy if it turns out to be a total lapse.
Goldman Sachs bank executives no longer receive cash rewards
U.S. Goldman Sachs senior executives won't receive cash rewards this year.
This was announced by a company respondinging to sharp criticism of its wage practices.
A group of 30 leading executives will be given shares instead of money that can be sold in five years at the earliest.
In addition, shares can be taken away from managers if they take excessive risks.
Goldman Sachs is thus at the forefront of efforts to combine rewards on Wall Street with long-term performance, according to Reuters.
I believe Wall Street is well aware of the broader direction it needs to take, said former JPMorgan investment banker Douglas Elliott.
The problem is in the details, he added.
Goldman Sachs became the target of criticism after putting aside nearly US$17bn (nearly $300bn) in rewards for the first three quarters of this year.
According to Reuters, the total amount of remuneration in the company for this year is likely to exceed $20bn, despite the measures announced today.
With the arrival of the financial crisis, high rewards in the banking sector have become a hot political issue in a number of countries.
Britain announced on Wednesday that it intends to burden bankers' bonuses in excess of 25,000 pounds (roughly 712,000 crowns) with a one-off 50 percent tax.
France is taking similar steps.
This year, women were awarded the Nobel Prize in all disciplines except physics
A record-breaking five women today in Stockholm took over this year's Nobel Prizes in professional categories and for literature.
In addition to the four scientists among them was the German writer of Romanian origin Herta Müllerová.
Two American biologists Elizabeth Blackburn and Carol Greider, along with their compatriot Jack Szostak, have been awarded the Nobel Prize in Medicine for their chromosome research.
The award for chemistry went to Israel's Ada Jonath, along with Americans Venkatraman Ramakrishnan and Thomas Steitz, for clarifying the structure and function of ribosomes.
The last laureate had the Nobel Prize in Economics, which was awarded to the American Elinor Ostrom and her compatriot Oliver Williamson for her analyses of economic administration.
The only vocational category that went without a holder this year was physics.
This award was awarded today by scientists Charles Kao for research in the field of optical fibers and George Smith with Willard Boyle for the invention of the CCD chip, which is the basis of all digital cameras, faxes or astronomical telescopes.
Each awardee has received a diploma, a Nobel Medal and a certificate of winning a financial reward.
For each category it reaches ten million Swedish crowns (about 25 million CZK).
If there are more awarded in each of them, they share the reward among themselves.
U.S. President Barack Obama received the most anticipated Nobel Peace Prize in Oslo, Norway, this afternoon.
In his speech, he acknowledged the controversy of his appreciation, because he is only at the beginning of his path and, moreover, is at the forefront of a country that wages two wars - in Iraq and Afghanistan.
In defending his policy, however, he added that wars are necessary in a certain situation to win peace, even if the price for this is high.
Minister Janota is considering his resignation. Klaus invited him to the Castle
President Vaclav Klaus will welcome Finance Minister Eduardo Janota on Friday morning, who is considering staying in the government because he disagrees with the shape of the state budget for next year, as approved by the Chamber of Deputies on Wednesday.
It is anticipated that the budget and possibly Janot's stay in the cabinet will be the themes of their Friday meeting.
Klaus also criticizes the budget.
According to him, it has too high a deficit, which further deepens the crisis of public finances.
The meeting was reported by the president's spokesperson Radim Ochvat.
Janota plans to meet with Prime Minister Jan Fischer on Monday.
According to the law on the budget, which Klaus now gets for signature, the Czech Republic should manage a deficit of 163 billion crowns.
On Wednesday, however, the House approved proposals from the left to move money and raised money for the salaries of civil servants, the operation of social services and direct payments to farmers.
The government criticized the Social Democrats' proposals for secretly increasing the budget deficit.
There was a sharp internal-political dispute between the left on the one hand and the government, Klaus and the right, on the other, because of this, because of which there is a beginning to raise a question mark over the further action of Fischer's official cabinet, the formation of which was agreed in the spring by the ODS, ČSSD and the Green Party.
Critics argue that the budget, as adopted by the House, is essentially liquidating a package of austerity measures that the Fischer government pushed through in the fall, which it said was intended to halt state indebtedness.
Klaus, which the law on the state budget may or may not sign, said at today's discussion breakfast with entrepreneurs that politicians have long neglected the "serious problem", which is the "unsustainable deficit" of public finances.
Today, according to him, he no longer has a good solution.
Only a strong government, which could rely on a broader consensus of political parties, could bring improvement.
ODS Chairman Mirek Topolánek said the budget was scary and the government should consider continuing to exist.
ODS Vice-President Petr Nečas told ČTK that the concept of an official cabinet with the support of ČSSD, ODS and greens is clearly no longer working.
In response, Prime Minister Fischer described similar words as "strong statements."
ČSSD boss Jiří Paroubek reacted to Topolánek by saying that the budget deficit, which the former Topolánek government has prepared for this year and which will exceed 200 billion crowns, is terrifying.
On Wednesday, the CSSD declared the approval of the state budget for next year to be a success.
The people were also satisfied.
Mandela was played by Morgan Freeman in Clint Eastwood
South Africans claim that the new Hollywood film Invictus: Undefeated will tell the world a lot about the country, its struggles and victories despite some criticism that the lead roles are portrayed by American actors.
The story tells of sports, racial circumstances and Nelson Mandela.
The relentless anti-apartheid racism campaigner in South Africa and its first black president, Nelson Mandela, in his life story features Oscar-winning American actor Morgan Freeman.
Freeman said ninety-one-year-old Mandela asked him if he could portray him in Eastwood's film.
Matt Damon plays an important role in it.
I told him, 'If I'm going to play you, I'll have to have access to you.
The seventy-two-year-old actor, who won an award for his role in Eastwood's Milion Dollar Baby boxing drama, has already portrayed, for example, the leader of the slave-abolitioning movement, who eventually became a slave, a fictional U.S. president and even a god in Divine Evan, but only rarely someone who is still alive and is as important to many people as Mandela.
The former South African statesman has been in prison for 27 years in prison for his active work for ending apartheid in South Africa.
He was freed in 1990 and became president of the country for four years.
In 1993 he was awarded the Nobel Peace Prize.
Invictus is the Latin word for the undefeated, and it's the title of a poem by English author William Ernest Henley published in 1875.
The challenge was to speak like him
The film is a true story of how Nelson Mandela joined forces with South African rugby team captain Francois Pienaaro to help unite the country. The newly elected President Mandela knows that his nation remains racially and economically divided after apartheid.
He believes he can connect his people through sport, so he decides to unite rugby players, who are among outsiders on a global scale.
The South African team will eventually advance to the World Cup in 1995.
Freeman worked for several years to get Mandela's story on screen.
I had no other goal than to play this role to bring it as close to reality as possible, Freeman said.
The biggest challenge, of course, was to speak like him.
The actor said that if he and the world politician were from the same area, he would try to meet Mandela, dine with him, be behind the scenes before his speech.
The most important thing was that he tried to shake Mandela's hand.
I've found that when I'm pressing your hand, I'm taking over your energy, it's transferring, and I felt like I knew how you felt, he said.
It's important for me to try to become another person.
Matt Damon portrays François Pienaaro, the captain of the national rugby team, dominated by white players.
The actor said he had six months to prepare for a role in the tough world of rugby.
A big surprise for him in the end was when he first met Pienaare at his house.
I remember ringing the house bell, he opened it for me, and the first thing I told François Pienaaro in my life was, 'I'm looking much bigger in the movie.'
Despite the obvious differences in the characters of the actors compared to their real-limed characters under director Eastwood, Invictus: Undefeated received positive reviews, and there's also talk of Oscar ambitions.
Todd McCarthy, a film critic of the Daily Variety, summarized his review: "It was a very good story, a very well-made film."
In film reviews on rottentomatoes.com, Invictus received 76 percent positive ratings.
The secret of the glow over Norse deciphered: the Russians tested the missile
It was possible to see a special light effect of unknown origin above Norway.
The Russian Defense Ministry only admitted yesterday that it tested an intercontinental ballistic missile near the Norwegian border.
However, the launch was unsuccessful again, and the missile referred to as "Bulava", which was originally supposed to be the pride of the Russian army, is slowly becoming a black moth of Russian generals and Prime Minister Vladimir Putin.
Even his repeated presence in missile tests did not have an appropriate effect on the success of test flights.
Bulava usually didn't take off at all or got damaged in the air.
In Russian newspapers, "Bulava" is referred to as "a rocket that does not fly".
A missile that doesn't fly but shines
This in the order of the 13th attempt took place normally at first.
At the end of the flight there was again technical damage to the apparatus.
Apparently, an engine exploded in the third stage of the rocket.
This time "Bulava" took off from the atomic submarine Dmitry Donskiy, which sailed below the surface of the White Sea.
This type of rocket allows you to shoot out of the vessel, which is moving and at the same time submerged.
Therefore, it is likely that the mysterious light above Norway caused by an unspecified flying object was indeed a malfunctioning "Bulava".
After all, the Norwegians had no doubt from the first moment that it was a Russian missile.
This time, the generals are defending themselves, claiming that it is impossible to unequivocally talk about failure.
The first two stages of the rocket lasted, and the accident hit only the third.
In previous cases, the engines broke down in the first instance.
Even though not a single test start took place without an error, only six out of thirteen consider it unsuccessful.
That’s why the general’s almost 50 percent success is filled with optimism, and he’s still claiming that “Bulava” will fly without any problems, and he’ll even carry up to ten hypersonic nuclear units with a total weight of 1.15 tons.
The legendary Fetisov signed a one-year contract at CSKA Moscow at 51
The legendary defender Vyacheslav Fetisov will once again present himself in a professional hockey match at the age of 51.
The former world champion, Olympic champion and Stanley Cup winner will help his CSKA Moscow team in an emergency and will probably play in Friday’s KHL duel against St. Petersburg.
Fetisov, who retired at the age of forty in 1998, is currently the president of CSKA.
After Denis Kulyash got injured, we urgently need another defender.
Fetisov trains regularly and agreed to help the team.
We just need to sort out some formalities, according to Sergei Nemchinov, the coach of the Moscow club.
It's not yet clear whether the famous hockey player's comeback will relate to just one game.
I think Fetisov is one of those who can raise the spirit of the other players, Nemchinov said.
Fetisov will become Russia's oldest professional hockey player, according to the AP, in the event of returning to the ice.
At the age of fifty-on-one in the NHL, legendary forward Gordie Howe scored 15 goals and 26 assists for Hartford.
The oldest of the famous five Fetisov, Kasatonov - Makarov, Larionov, Krutov is one of the best and most successful hockey players in history.
In the jersey of the Soviet Union won two Olympic golds, seven world championship titles, he triumphed at the Canadana Cup as well as the junior world championships.
In the second part of his career, he also gained success in the NHL, where he lifted the Stanley Cup twice in the Detroit jersey.
He has been a member of the Hockey Hall of Fame since 2001.
Hyunsa has complied with unions, abolishing all overtime
The management of Nošovice automaker Hyundai has agreed with the unions to cancel all overtime.
It is precisely because of the often ordered overtime that unions declared a strike emergency on Monday.
This was confirmed by Hyundai spokesman Petr Vaněk.
However, the management wants to agree with trade unionists on one more of Saturday's shifts this year.
We cannot resign ourselves to our production plan, which is essential, that's whay voluntary overtime was proposed on Saturday, December 19, saying that two shifts would be made, Vaněk said.
News server Sedmička.cz wrote that the company of employees who work Saturday's shift offered a bonus of 400 Kč.
People will get it retrospectively and on a working Saturday, November 28.
According to Vaněk, the company decided to change the way to motivate employees for the proposed overtime.
They can either reimburse an overtime surcharge or they can choose to take a spare holiday on December 28, thus extending the Christmas holidays for a day, Vaněk said.
In addition, all employees of Saturday's shift will receive a paid fare and lunch.
Given that overtime was the most urgent and most pressing point in terms of comments and complaints from trade unionists, as well as protesting employees who spontaneously halted production last week, the management decided with immediate effect yesterday afternoon to cancel all overtime for the month of December, Vaněk said.
Until last week, employees of the automaker were forced to work two-hour overtime almost every day after an eight-hour shift.
After signing the agreement, the unions will call off the strike emergency
Further talks between the automaker's management and trade unions are due to take place on Friday morning.
Trade union chief Petr Kuchař said on Wednesday that if they agree to meet the demands and sign a joint document, they are ready to cancel the strike emergency.
The situation at the automaker escalated last week on Wednesday, when roughly 400 employees spontaneously went on strike because of constant overtime.
They also objected to poor pay conditions and alleged bullying.
The management countered that overtime must be done by employees to meet the requirements for car deliveries.
Trade unions, in Monday's announcement of the strike alert, also demanded that non-sanctions be sanctioned by employees who discontinued production last Wednesday.
In the area of overtime, unions require the company to ease overtime work to a minimum.
In addition, they want to pay 5000 crowns for it this year.
Nošovice automaker Hyundai now employs 2000 people.
The company started mass production last November.
By this September it had produced about 80,000 cars, its current production capacity is 200,000 cars a year.
Czech discovery: a substance that works even for 'hardened' HIV viruses
A team of Czech and German scientists is testing new compounds that can slow the spread of HIV in the body.
The main advantage of the improved substance is that it also occupies on viruses that have already acquired immunity against drugs.
In some cases, this fact outweighs the fact that the compound does not affect the common variant of the virus as strongly as some already available drugs.
AIDS has not yet been cured.
However, it can significantly prolong patients' lives with a mixture of drugs that prevent the multiplication of HIV in the body.
However, their use is accompanied by a number of side effects.
In addition, incomplete suppression of the multiplication of the virus results in the development of so-called resistant viruses, against which drugs are already ineffective.
The work of experts from three institutes of the Academy of Sciences of the Czech Republic, from the University of Technology in Prague and from the University of Heidelberg opens the way to cope with the resistance of the virus.
They have shown that substances called metalocarborates have an effect on the protein responsible for the multiplication of HIV virus.
Metalocarborates are compounds of boron, hydrogen, carbon and cobalt.
These compounds block the spread of the virus differently than all drugs used today, and therefore they can overcome the problem of resistance.
In their work, scientists come up with new ‘improved’ compounds prepared based on knowledge of the molecular mechanism of their attachment to viral protein.
Metalocarboras have a unique three-dimensional structure: two multi-walled cages made up of boron, hydrogen and carbon atoms are connected by a metal atom, in this case cobalt.
Treacherous and resilient virus
HIV protease is an HIV protein that is necessary for the life cycle of the virus.
Without HIV proteasis caused by cleavagage of HIV, mature infectious viral particles would not occur.
When we stop HIV protease, we also stop the spread of the virus in the patient’s body, the researchers explain in a press release issued by the Institute of Organic Chemistry and Biochemistry of the Academy of Sciences of the Czech Republic.
In a paper published in the Journal of Medicinal Chemistry, the researchers describe a series of compounds in which two pairs of cages (see table) are linked by a short organic chain that is further systematically altered.
A little weaker, but more reliable
The effect of this series of HIV protease substances has been tested in the test tube and also against its resistant (resistant) variants derived from patients infected with HIV.
The effect of metalocarborans on a common variant of the enzyme is not as strong as with clinically used drugs, but it does not lose its effect for resistant variants against which the drugs used are often ineffective.
The unique mechanism of action and their other properties such as biological and chemical stability, low toxicity and the possibility of other chemical modifications make metallocarborans interesting compounds for further research aimed at the design of effective drugs against HIV, says Pavlína ŘezáčovÁ, head of the Laboratory of Structural Biology of the Institute of Organic Chemistry and Biochemistry and the Institute of Molecular Genetics of the Academy of Sciences.
An argument over the budget. ODS embarked on Fischer, who defends himself
The head of the ODS, Mirek Topolánek, described the approved budget for next year as "frightening" and the government of Prime Minister Jan Fischer, according to him, should consider another existence.
The ODS's remarks were described by the prime minister as strong statements.
He's giving himself time for analysis.
ODS Vice-President Petr Nečas said that the concept of an official cabinet with the support of the ČSSD, ODS and the Green Party is clearly no longer working.
According to Nečas, the prime minister must decide whether he intends to rely on a minority in the Chamber of Deputies, which is about fighting a growing deficit, or if he wants to remain in office with the help of the parliamentary left.
The approach of the ODS and social democracy on the issue of public deficits is diametrically different, Nečas pointed out.
On Wednesday, the House adopted a budget for 2010, but with left-wing amendments for more than 12 billion crowns.
The House raised money for the salaries of civil servants, for the operation of social services and direct payments to farmers.
At the same time, the government's rebuke to the Social Democrat's proposals was that they were secretly increasing the budget deficit.
The Czech Republic should manage a deficit of 163 billion crowns.
The ODS left the hall before the vote, thus complying with the government's wishes that even an amended budget is less of a bad option than the budgetary provisional.
Disappointment and vanity
The Minister of Finance, Eduard Janota, spoke about the disappointment and a sense of futility, Prime Minister Jan Fischer said that the government would still evaluate the current situation.
Nečas is convinced that if Janota were to seriously threaten to leave office in the event of the adoption of amendments, the situation could have looked different.
According to Czech Television, Janota made an appointment with the prime minister for Monday after the government.
He indicated that he was considering remaining in office.
The reason for this is his disappointment with the budget negotiations.
If Fischer speaks out in favour of a further fight with the budget deficit, according to Nečas, ČSSD nominees, who pushed through the changes in the budget, have nothing to do in the government.
The Prime Minister should also make it clear that he will resign if the House approves other deficit-enhancinging laws, such as those relating to signiting, pensions, or an amendment to the Service Act.
Topolánek: ODS nominees not guilty
It's a matter for Prime Minister Fischer and the whole government.
ODS nominees did nothing wrong, Topolánek stressed in a text message from the U.S.
He reminded that in several ministries there are people in deputy functions reporting to TOP 09, who voted against the budget.
Nečas emphasized that if Fischer decides to abandon the principle of combating the amount of public deficits, "it is perfectly logical and correct to lean on the left-wing majority, which is currently in the Chamber of Deputies".
However, it cannot count on the support and tolerance of Civic Democrats.
I didn't think about it, and when it's up-to-date, I'll deal with it, defense minister Martin Barták told reporters in the Chamber of Deputies, who was nominated by civic democrats.
In the afternoon, Prime Minister Fischer flew to Brussels at a European Council meeting.
At the request of the Prime Minister, the ODS retreated
At the same time, Nečas stressed that the ODS would probably vote against an amended budget in the House on Wednesday if the prime minister did not explicitly ask the Democrat MPs to allow the adoption of the budget.
We don't feel like orphans, we're not small children, then he responded to the question of whether the ODS was not burdened by the absence of the party's chairman in both parliament and the Czech Republic during the political budget negotiations.
I didn't see that my voice was missing.
Even when I gave up my mandate, I knew that in the coming months it would be primarily the Bolshevization of the Chamber of Deputies and the destruction of everything positive, Topolánek himself reacted.
According to the new ČD order, fewer trains will run for almost the same fare
From 13 December, the Czech Railways will cancel or limit some less busy express trains and locales, while other connections will be strengthened.
The total scope of transport will be reduced by two percent from the date of validity of the new timetable compared to today.
Most of the fares, including basic and customer fares, won't change.
For example, residents of Ostrava will get more opportunities to connect to the capital, the runways will add one pendolino train to this track.
On the other hand, the high-speed train will stop driving to Bratislava.
Fewer express trains will also depart on the Prague - Písek - České Budějovice line, fundamentally limiting the direct connections of Prague - Letohrad.
Of the regions, railways are planning the largest reduction of transport in the Hradec Králové Region, by a total of eight percent, on the contrary, Prague and its surroundings can look forward to the increase.
Two night trains on the route Prague - Tábor - České Budějovice are also cancelled and the operation of several express trains on the line Prague - Písek - České Budějovice is limited only to some days of the week.
One of the biggest changes is the introduction of a new direct line from Milovice to Prague, today Milovická has to transfer in Lysá nad Labem.
It is the fifteenth line of the suburban Esko system, its opening is possible thanks to the completion of the electrification of the line from Lysá nad Labem to Milovice.
Long-distance connections to the capital will end at the Prague Main Station.
ČD will introduce more SC Pendolino connections between Prague and Ostrava, and trains to Šumperk/Jeseník will run at two-hour intervals.
Passengers will no longer be transported to Bratislava by pendoline since 13 December.
This year's account for regional railways: 200 million more
The speedsters are ordered and paid by the Ministry of Transport, in total for the payment of the so-called demonstrable loss will give four billion crowns next year, as well as this year. Locals are ordered by regions, in total eight billion crowns will be paid for next year, while less than three billion will be contributed by the state.
This year, regional trains are 200 million more expensive.
There is a newly concluded ten-year contract for locals and speedsters, so far the contract has always been for one year.
Railways praise the system, which is probably why they have not reached for an increase in the prices of the vast majority of tickets.
There will be changes only for one-day ČD Net network tickets and cheap eLiška internet tickets.
For passengers without a customer card, ČD Net’s price will increase from 450 crowns to 600 crowns.
The price of eLiška will now depend on the distance driven, so far it has been unified for the journey between arbitrary regional towns and amounted to 160 crowns.
Philippine gunmen still detains more than 50, children released
Militants today kidnapped 75 people in the southern Philippines, including several elementary school pupils and their teachers.
This was reported by AFP, which initially reported only 65 abductees.
All 17 detained children were discharged after eight hours, including one teacher.
The kidnapping took place in Agusan del Sur province.
Approximately 19 militants are said to be using hostages as a living shield while fleeing from police.
Local negotiators are now trying to agree to extradite the remaining hostages.
According to AFP, the militants belong to the New People's Army (NPA), which is an armed component of the Communist Party of the Philippines (CPP).
According to the Associated Press, police initially pursued the kidnappers because of the violence, which necessated the settlement of accounts between two local family clans.
Martial law has been proclaimed in the southern Philippines in Maguindanao province because of a recent massacre in which 57 people were killed.
Among the Victims of the November 23 massacre related to elections to the governor's office were 30 journalists.
On suspicion of involvement in the massacre, the governor of this Jihofilipine province and his father, Andal Ampatuan, the patriarch of the influential Ampatuan family, were arrested.
Muslim separatists are also active on Mindanao.
However, they resumed peace talks with the Philippine government on Tuesday.
The story of JAR affects everyone, says Morgan Freeman alias Mandela
Over the next few days, Clint Eastwood Invictus's new Hollywood cinema is coming to U.S. Cinemaries, which captures part of the life story of former South African President Nelson Mandela.
The role of the world-renowned anti-apartheid campaigner was played by Morgan Freeman, whore Mandela has chosen for his portrayal years ago.
Matt Damon played the male role of the legendary South African rugby champion Francoise Pienaaro.
With the introduction of the film to cinemas arises from the responses of both critics and the public
Nelson Mandela
Nelson Rolihlahla Mandela was born on July 18, 1918 in Mvez, South Africa.
In 1988 he received the Sakharov Prize for Freedom of Thought, and in 1939, together with de Klerk, the Nobel Peace Prize.
He was president of South Africa from 1994-1999.
high expectations, because it comes to the public at a time when preparations for a world football championship are culminating in South Africa, but at the same time the media is shaken by disturbing reports of increasing racial hatred and discrimination against the population by the black ruling majority.
According to the Associated Press, the testimonials for the new film in South Africa are overwhelmingly positive for the time being, despite numerous reservations about casting the main roles by Hollywood stars.
Morgan Freeman, however, is proud of the new film.
I've dreamed of that role for many years and I've been preparing well for it.
I have also meted Nelson Mandela many times in person to - as they say - recharge his energy, he stated in an interview after the film's ceremonial premiere in Los Angeles.
I think it's a good thing if we can recall Mandela's personality and message through the film.
It's South Africa's story, but it concerns every person, he added.
In the jersey of the national rugby team
Based on the book by British writer John Carlin with the loosely translated Role of the Enemy: Nelson Mandela and the play that gave birth to the nation portrays a South African human rights defender during his first presidential term.
Morgan Freeman thus tries to bring together two races during the course of the film and, for many years, mutually defined groups of the population, and just like Nelson Mandela in 1995, he enters in an acting role in the South African national rugby jersey on the field of the World Cup, which was then won by South Africa.
For entering the lawn in the jersey, which in South Africa is almost like something sacred, Mandela reaped a great ovation of a predominantly white audience fourteen years ago and gained the recognition of the white population.
The football championship may be able to reunite people into one nation, said rugby player Chester Williams, who was the only "black" in the national team in 1995.
Whether the tense situation in JAR will improve is yet to be seen.
However, white South Africans are skeptical of how in a recent LN interview Christopher Dawson, a rugby fan who fled the country to Britain, revealed.
The championship will begin on June 11, 2010.
Transformer burns in Lounsko for tens of millions of crowns
In the morning, Čeps transformer engulfed the fire in a substation near the municipality of Výškov in Lounsko.
According to Lukáš Marvan, a spokesperson for regional firefighters in Ústí nad Labem, six units intervened in the fire.
Originally, firefighters wanted to leave the transformer burned out, but later reconsidered the opinion and the fire was extinguished by a mixture of water, foam and special extinguishing agents.
This was caused by a technical fault, an internal short-circuit of the transformer.
No one has been injured, according to the company's spokeswoman Pavla Mandátová, the crash does not endanger the safe operation of the transmission system.
The damage is estimated at several tens of millions of crowns, precisely quantifying it will succeed only after the end of the fire.
According to the Mandate, the damaged transformer will have to be replaced by a new one.
Nothing similar has happened in the ten years of Čeps, defects of this type are unique, she added.
According to CEZ spokeswoman Soni Holingerová Hendrychová, the supply of electricity has not been interrupted, the functioning of households, authorities and companies has not been affected by the transformer station fire.
A.s. Čeps operates in the territory of the Czech Republic as the exclusive operator of the transmission system, power lines 400 kV and 220 kV.
Maintains, restores and develops 39 substations with 67 transformers converting electrical energy from the transmission to the distribution system and the 4339-kilometre line route.
Jančura will file a criminal complaint for defamation against three CSSD governors
Student Agency owner Radim Jančura will file a criminal complaint today for defamation against three social-democratic governors.
Michal Hašek from the South Moravian Region, Radko Martínek from the Pardubice Region and Martin Tesařík, Governor of the Olomouc Region.
Jančura stated this in Twenty Minutes of Radiožurnál.
The lawsuit relates to the company's efforts to operate regional train transport in some regions.
However, all regions eventually signed long-term contracts with Czech Railways.
All three governors are attacking us and talking about choosing raisins, while we have brought an offer only for what they wanted.
He claims that we are more expensive than Czech Railways, which is not true, because we know the prices of Czech Railways, which were closed last week.
So we have no choice but to defend ourselves.
It's for defamation towards the Student Agency, Jančura told Radiožurnál.
At the same time, he said he had evidence from all counties that they had not taken their actions in accordance with European law.
It therefore prepares an initiative for the European Commission.
Hašek considers filing a criminal complaint as a further continuation of Mr. Jančura's media show, which serves as unpaid advertising by his companies and his own person.
Of course, I am ready to defend the good name of myself and my county, and I will invite other governors to do the same.
I assume that even on this occasion it will become clear that it is Mr. Jančura who manipulates the public in the matter of regional rail transport and uses false and untrue arguments, Hašek said.
Jančura announced last week that it would invest several million crowns in advertising, which will draw attention to the disadvantageous step of the regions.
He told Radiožurnál that he would interrupt the campaign after Christmas and want to continue it after the New Year.
He specified that the campaign would be waged against corruption as such.
Jančura started the campaign after the regions agreed to operate regional railway connections with the Czech Railways and did not list tenders for a contract of around 150 billion crowns.
According to Jančura, such conduct is illegal and contrary to the opinion of the European Court of Justice.
Due to the criticism of train transport in the regions, Jančura was also threatened by an MP for the ČSSD.
The only threat was that if we didn't stop, we could lose bus licenses.
He introduced himself as a member of the ČSSD, I know which gentleman he is, I will not say his name, Jančura told Radiožurnál today.
Vaccination against pneumococci will be free and optional
Vaccination against pneumococci has been demolishing habits since January.
It should be the first for families to be free and optional at the same time.
However, there are already voices that mandatory vaccination would be more effective.
Preparations for the introduction of free pneumococcal vaccination are culminating.
The drug institute is already setting the maximum prices of vaccines paid by health insurance companies.
Experts are also clear about the vaccination process.
New Vaccinations
Free vaccines for august children
From January, anti-pneumococcal infants who were born on August 2 and later could be vaccinated free of charge.
The condition is that they have not yet received a single dose of vaccination.
Those who started vaccinating last or earlier have to pay for other revaccinations themselves.
The first dose of vaccination must be given between 3 and 5 months of age.
Last year, a quarter of families voluntarily had their children vaccinated against pneumococci.
Parents paid 1600 crowns for one dose or more.
Four doses are needed to vaccinate the youngest children.
Overall, experts estimate that health insurance companies spend 300 to 450 thousand crowns a year on vaccine doses.
However, it saves on the treatment that children without vaccinations would need.
So far, the state has given about 500 thousand a year for all blanket vaccination of children.
The paid vaccination was introduced by the so-called Janot package.
Pneumococcus causes inflammation of the middle ear, but also serious infections including inflammation of the brain membranes and blood poisoning.
According to hospitals, up to 28 children under the age of 10 will die from pneumococcal infections in a year.
In addition to the Czech Republic, they have introduced paid blanket vaccination of children against pneumococci in about 40 countries.
Will vaccination be mandatory?
At the same time, there are opinions that this vaccination could be mandatory in the future.
From January, it is supposed to be paid by insurance companies and optional.
Rudolf Kalovský, the head of the Nahlas civic association, suggested that vaccination would be better, for example.
His association has been promoting pneumococcal vaccines for a long time.
Experts at the international conference, which was attended by our association, were not entirely satisfied with the fact that we have voluntary vaccination.
It's not exactly ideal.
On the other hand, we are glad that we managed to achieve at least this.
I think it's the path we have to take now, Kalovský said.
The Ministry of Health, however, does not want to change the procedure of vaccination against pneumococci.
We're not even considering it at the moment, his spokesman, Vlastimil Sršeň, said.
Voluntary vaccination has its drawbacks, doctors say
Hana Cabrnochová, the president of the Professional Society of Pediatricians, recognizes that voluntary vaccination has its disadvantages.
If all children were vaccinated, it could be considered to reduce the number of boosters "In a situation where this vaccine costs such a high amount, we fairly understand the political decision that the vaccination was started as voluntary," Cabrnochová says.
Experts expect more than 80 percent of fresh parents to vaccinate their infants against pneumococci next year.
Vaccination against pneumococci is groundbreaking even in the fact that it is the first blanket vaccination of children paid for by health insurance companies, not by the state.
Insurance companies should issue money for prevention, thus saving for further treatment, Cabrnochová explains.
Doctors will have to buy fabrics themselves
However, this is related to the concerns of general pediatricians concerning the purchase of vaccines.
According to the original intention, vaccines were supposed to be bought by insurance companies, and doctors would receive them in the same way as the state supplies them with substances for other vaccinations.
However, some MPs were concerned that insurance companies could "take money out of health insurance."
Therefore, doctors will have to buy fabrics and wait for insurance companies to reimburse them.
According to estimates, individual practical children's doctors will invest 40 thousand a month in vaccines, which is a significant sum.
It won't be easy at all, Cabrnoch says.
American artist's son 'stolen at museum'
The son of renowned American fantastics creator Frank Frazetta has been charged with the attempted robbery of paintings worth $20 million (12 million pounds) from his father's museum.
Police in Pennsylvania said Alfonso Frank Frazetta had been caught loading 90 paintings into his vehicle and trailer.
Appointed Mr. Frazetta and the other man used excavators to break into a museum in the Poconco Mountains region.
Mr Frazetta senior, aged 81, became famous for the creation of characters Barbara Conan and Tarzan.
By the time it happened, the artist was located in Florida, according to the Associated Press news agency.
The AP quoted an unnamed police official who said Mr. Frazetta Jr could have been motivated by long-running family controversies.
In front of the police, Mr Frazetta Jr. issued an affidavit, the age of 52, that he did so at his father's order "to reach the museum in any way possible and move the paintings to the malting premises," according to the agency.
Mr Frazetta senior has denied granting any such permission, as reported by the agency.
Labour is resisting a planned rise in taxes
Ministers advocated an increase in taxes and a reduction in spending, announced in a preliminary report on the budget against criticism by the opposition, businessmen and trade unions.
According to Alistair Darling, the Tories "squandered" the opportunity to show they were serious about postponing the deficit-reduction decision until after the election.
The chancellor, too, came under fire due to low- and middle-income workers' disabilities.
According to his proposal, this is mainly an increase of 0.5 % in social insurance and a 1% adjustment of public payments since 2011.
Distinguishment about Social Security
Unions protested that low-income workers were punished for a recession they did not cause and warned in advance of "problems."
Social insurance is increasing - an increase of around 3 trillion pounds a year - outraging a community of entrepreneurs who said it was a tax on employment, while there should be a focus on rebuilding the economy.
This increase, limited to these revenues of more than 20000 pounds a year, will affect around 10 million workers.
KEY POINTS PBR
Social insurance will increase by an additional 0.5% from April 2011
The economic situation will worsen more than expected, by 4.75% this year
New taxation of 50 per cent bankers' bonuses
An increase of 1% tax on companies for depreciations for small businesses
Reducing the tax on electric cars and wind farms
Increasing the assessment of pensions by 2.5%
According to an estimate by the state's tax administration, some revenue of 30000 pounds will decrease by 90 pounds a year, and some revenue of 40000 pounds will decrease by 190 pounds, while some revenue of 10000 pound will increase by 110 pounds.
Ministers announced that their deficit reduction target meant "complicated decision-making" by 2013, but insisted that 60% of special taxes should fall to 5% of those with the highest incomes.
Treasury Secretary Stephen Timms denied that the proposed increase in taxes and spending cuts were a "drop in the ocean" compared to what would have to be done.
"These are big numbers, which will allow us to reduce the deficit in half, which is an absolutely basic requirement for the next four years," he told the BBC.
Mr Timms said he was confident the growth of the economy should happen at the turn of the year.
But developing a forecast for future economic growth - which will affect the volume of necessary loans and how quickly the deficit will be reduced - must be taken into account after the chancellor has been forced to revise earlier amounts.
In his opinion, he said, the downturn in the economy this year was 4.75% - far worse than a decline of 3.5%, predicted in April - because the loans were 3 trillion pounders higher than previously estimated.
Wasted opportunity'
Conservatives, who launched a new promotional campaign on Thursday warning of Labour's "indebtedness crisis," described the planned 789 trillion pound loan over the next six years as intolerable.
Ministers have failed in presenting a credible plan for how this could be reimbursed, added, "cynically" postponed this decision until after the election - which must take place by June 2010.
"They didn't add anything significant that would have been feasible," shadow finance minister Philip Hammond said in a statement. "They had the opportunity to do it, but they squandered it."
“All important announcements will be made after the election if we win them ”
Roger Bootle, Deloitte
Labour insists that schools, hospitals and police will be shielded from future cuts in contrast to the Tory's proposals, who would like to reduce the deficit more quickly and to a greater extent.
But Liberal Democrats argue that money raised by raising taxes and cutting spending should be used to support spending next year and not to reduce the deficit, arguing that the plans are "built on the sand."
BBC political columnist Nick Robinson said about the timing of the communication - for example, the above-mentioned inflation will increase and there will be an inability to pay benefits next April - that this could lead to chancellor's accusations in the election campaign if the money was difficult to recover a year later.
But Labour insists on providing special assistance if people need it very much and the situation is revised in September 2010.
Suspicious budget'
Although ministers did not disclose details of spending after 2011, economists said it was clear that a reduction in public spending would be only the beginning of medium-term restrictions.
The acknowledged Institute of Budgetary Studies said the chancellor's digits suggest substantial cuts in many areas, potentially involving transportation, higher education and defense in the future.
"It's actually very painful so far," his director, Robert Chote, said.
"All the important statements will come only after the election, if we win them," added Roger Bootle, an economic adviser to Deloitte's account reviror.
"That's a report on a suspicious pre-budget. Markets are implementing it."
The FBI is conducting investigations into 'U.S. prisons in Pakistan'
The FBI is conducting investigations at a Pakistan prision with five Americans suspected of links to extremists.
The men have been jailed for attacking a house in Sargoda in eastern Punjab province, according to the US embassy in Pakistan.
The FBI said it was an investigation into whether they were the same men who were pronounced missing last month in their residence in the U.S. state of Virginia.
The U.S. State Department said it was also about getting information about these men.
Three of them are said to be originally from Pakistan, one of them has Egyptian ancestors, and the others come from Yemen.
“If they were U.S. citizens, we’d be very interested in what conditions they were detained and under what circumstances they were staying there.”
FBI spokeswoman Katherine Schweit said the agency had been informed of imprisonment and had been in contact with the families of the missing students.
"We are cooperating with the Pakistani authorities to determine their identity and the nature of their actions here and whether they are really missing students," she said.
The Pakistani embassy in Washgington said the men had been arrested at a house belonging to the uncle of one of them.
It stated that the house was always the focus of interest of the local police and that charges had not yet been brought against the arrested men.
Secretary of State Hillry Clinton declined to comment on the arrests, according to Reuters, but said the United States must "cooperate much more closely with Afghanistan and Pakistan in uncovering the infrastructure of terrorism, which continues to recruit and train individuals."
The disappearance of these five students from a home in northern Virginia was announced by their families at the end of November.
Rejecting an amnesty for the Iranian mistreated.
The situation in respect for human rights in Iran is as bad as in the last 20 years, according to a report by Amnesty International.
The report details "ways of torture" in mode before and after a disputed presidential election in June.
One of the men said he was beaten and burned with cigarettes. Another said he was threatened with rape.
Iran has denied the above criticism of this report on respect for human rights.
Officials described the criticism as politically motivated.
Thousands of people have been imprisoned in Iran and dozens have been killed after the disputed election of President Mahmoud Ahmadinejad, led to the largest street demonstrations since the 1979 Islamic Revolution.
Dozens of them were tied to detention, and prosecutors sentenced the last five people to death.
Tehran's B.B.O.M. Corrector John Leyne, who is now located in London, said earlier in protests Supreme Leader Ayyatolah Ali Khomeini had declared the unlegitimacy of certain modes of torture and banned their implementation at Karizak's correctional facility.
But since then, almost no criticism of legal power has been tolerated, as our correspondent said.
Untrue Confessions'
Amnesty International has quoted a 26-year-old computer student Ibrahim Methari as saying he has been charged for "working with the Facebook network" and for protests against the election results.
"Often I've been beaten in the face," he said.
"They burned me with cigarettes under my eyes, on my neck, my head... they threatened me with execution and humiliated me."
After five days, he signed an untrue confession and was released and left in the street, still bleeding and half unconscious, Amnesty said.
In August, defeated presidential candidate Mehdi Charroubí was detained after the protests after the election and was tortured to death in prison and the others were raped.
Iran's senior civil servants have denied allegations of rape, but have admitted that torture took place.
Amnesty also cited a case of a former inmate who said it had been held in a container with another 75 people for more than eight weeks at Kahrizak prison.
Amnesty has acknowledged that Iran's parliament and justices have set up post-election riot investigation committees and the government's responses, but said mandates and authority powers were unclear and the findings of parliamentary committees had been published.
The group said at least 90 people have been detained in the past three weeks to prevent further demonstrations.
A ski slope has been created in the city park
More than 100 tonnes of artificial snow was used to turn a landmark in Bristol into a downhill track for the race.
The downhill track for the race in the park attracted 16 semi-professional skiers and snowboarders to compete for financial prizes.
At the top of the downhill in Brandon Hill Park there was a short skating bridge in front of the 100-meter-long downhill track.
The fourth-year medical student John Hickman said it was a wonderful view from the top of the downhill track, but that it was really daunting.
It was a wonderful view of the blinking lights of the city below.
There have been some of the best skiers and snowboarders from all over the country - some of them real talents," he added.
Prizes were awarded for the best pieces - or maneuvers on the downhill track.
After Thursday night's race, the snow was left to melt.
Still them and us
Can a Muslim be a Frenchman?
Pluralism and pragmatism in a secular state.
At a time when Swiss voters were calling for a ban on the construction of minarets and generally spreading alarms about the alleged Islamization of Europe, John Bowen, an American academic, wrote and reported, reflecting on whether Muslims could integrate - and integrate - into one of the continent's most open secular societies.
Some of the readers became admirers of the author's last work after reading this book, "Why the French don't like headscarves" (2006), this elegant and discussed study on contentious issues that have been dividing and employing the country for 15 years and whose impacts are still being felt today.
Mr. Bowen's latest book has a wider and much more ambitious scope.
As a good anthropologist, he wants to know not only what politicians and the media have said about Islam in France, but what has really happened.
He's spent months in mosques, schools and institutes, which now care for 5 ÷ 6 million French Muslims, which Mr. Bowen calls "Islamic spaces."
He's a good listener, reproducing debates between teachers and students about the issues that worry them the most.
Should a Muslim enter a marital union in a mosque or town hall (or in both places)?
Should a young Muslim be taught about the development and rights of gays?
Can a Muslim woman get married to a man who isn’t Muslim?
Does a Muslim have the right to use an interest-based banking system if he wants to get a mortgage?
It's these seemingly secular contentious issues that are being debated, and which are part of everyday life rather than the political dramas employed by the media.
The author identifies a new generation of imams, teachers and intellectuals, among other well-known persons, including Tarik Ramadan, an Arab, a Muslim researcher and academic born in Switzerland.
This new generation is attempting to open a discussion about how to be at the same time a good Muslim and a good citizen in a modern secular society.
They don't have arguments for all their customs.
Conservatives are incredulous about the true idea of French or European Islam.
Philosophers and activists with whom Mr. Bowen conducted an interrogation are inclined to believe that he is the opposite of Selafi—the supporters of the Purist Sunni Islam, associated in Saudi Arabia—this group is small, but affects European Muslims.
Mr. Bowen believes that Muslim values and French secularism are compatible.
But customization requires compromise on both sides.
He wonders how much French politicians (and the intellectual elite who so stubbornly advocates the lay public) are truly committed to the idea of pluralism.
It is of the opinion that Muslims probably have less experience in matters of faith than Catholics, Protestants and Jews, who also had to make historical compromises with secular republicanism.
Rather than developing pragmatism, he found "tightening the screws of values."
Can a Muslim be a Frenchman? After reading this book, someone may tend to say "Yes, but not now."
The world's most bleak prospects
The arctic is changing more dramatically than the rest of the planet's environment.
Bounding ice melts at an alarming rate, which affects animals and their survival like nowhere else.
The interests of oiling, gas and fishing companies must focus on new natural sources of water, together with diplomats, lawyers and writers upon their awakening.
In "On Thin Ice," Richard Ellis, a writer and illustrator, recounts the natural history of an icon of the North, a polar bear.
Well acquainted with the complicated history and practices of whaling describes the long tradition of polar explorers who tried to seize a white bear.
Admiral Nelson's encounter with a courageous fourteen-year-old lifeguard who struggled only with a round of musketics is indeed a myth, but everything else is true.
