6000 Marines laid wreaths on graves in Arlington
Petty Officer De'Angello Robinson, 19, traveled seven hours by bus to lay a simple wreath on a soldier's grave he had never encountered.
For him and the other Marines who made the journey from Camp Johnson in Jacksonville in North Carolina to the National Cemetery in Arlington to adorn graves, this was a day in which they paid tribute to men and women who once wore the same shoes.
In a few months when we're boarding, it could be us, Robinson said.
If this happens to us, I would like someone to do the same for me in such a way that he will show me his respect as I am trying.
He was one of more than 6,000 volunteers who gathered that Saturday morning to lay wreaths at Veterans Cemetery in several wards during a commemorative ceremony in Arlington.
In 1992, Morrill Worcester, the owner of Worcester's Funeral Society in Harrington, Maine, began this tradition when he and several others decided to decorate a few hundred graves during a commemorative act in Arlington.
It's now been an 18-year-old trade, with Mr. nMorill and his wife Karen making this journey every year and stopping in various cities along the way to honor the memory of members of the military and the victims of terrorism.
Worcesters are not looking for profit, wreaths across the Americas, which they also consider in other states.
That year, volunteers laid more than 16,000 wreaths at Arlington Cemetery, the Pentagon territory, graves at the Fayetteville National Cemetery in Arkansas, at Battery Park in New York City, and at the memorial site of flight number 93 United Airlines in Shanksville, Pennsylvania.
For the second year, Walmart Foundation has donated more than 150000 dollars for the purchase and transportation of wreaths.
Charleen Hunt, a 70-year-old from Westminster, said she had made a trip to Arlington in honor of her husband, a deceased member of the armed forces.
Because her husband isn't buried in Arlington, Ms. Hunt said it was her way of returning what the victim was doing.
It's a small way citizens can support our troops, she said.
It's kind of like visiting the family grave.
Shrouded in cloaks of warm cloth, volunteers passed through the graveyard on surrounding lawns and hills.
Volunteers headed for older graves from the first and second world wars and from the Vietnam War, because these aren't visited as often as newer graves
The mood was positive when the children played and held their hands together as they transported the young pine tree to the grayed-up tombstones.
Each circular wreath was dark green with a small red arch over the upper part.
Neighborhood§ in Department 60, where the majority of veterans from the wars in Iraq and Afghanistan are buried, there has indeed been silence.
Associated service organizations donated 1000 wreaths to adorn the department, but the gloomy mood of the current wars fulfilled this area.
Some wept sincerely, others prayed, and families and friends kept each other in the cold air.
Some read names aloud, engraved in stones, while others bent down to inspect small black-and-while namescales, pushed into wet mud where tombstones had not yet been placed.
Sandra Lockwood was one of the many mothers who wiped tears from their eyes in front of their son's grave.
She rode for eight hours from Zanesfield, Ohio, to visit the final resting place of nautical artillery sergeant David Shane Spicero, who fell in combat in July.
No one should ever forget why we're single . . .my son paid for it, she said.
"Even though I've been away for a long time, I want to be the one to remember him."
