The Cuban government has jailed a U.S. government supplier who imported cellphones and laptops into the country, as State Department officials said Saturday.
The supplier, who has not yet been identified, has worked for Development Alternatives Inc. headquartered in Bethesda.
The company is working on projects for the U.S. Agency for International Development and the World Bank.
Consular officials, along with the U.S. interest section in Havana, were seeking access to the detainee who had been jailed on December 5.
Certain allegations have not been made public, but under Cuban law, a Cubanic citizen or foreigner can be jailed for almost anything labeled "dangerous."
All so-called counter-revolutionary activities, which include moderate protests and criticial stats, raise the risk of imprisonment.
Anti-governmental graffiti and speech are considered serious offences.
There's a young blogging community in Cuba, led by popular commentator Yoani Sánchez, who often writes about how she and her husband are tracked and harassed by government agents.
Ms Sánchez has repeatedly asked for permission to leave the country so that she can accept prizes, but she has been denied permission.
Detaining a U.S. contractor may be the result of rising tensions between Castro's communist brother, ruling in Cuba, and the Obama administration, which has been tracked as a "delaying tactic" in improving relations with the island.
They were the first to file reports of the New York Times imprisonment Friday night.
The new U.S. policy highlighted that if the Cuban government takes concrete steps, such as the release of political prisoners and creates more space for the opposition, the United States will take reciprocal steps.
Mobile phones and laptops are allowed in Cuba, although they are new and sought-after commodities in a country where the average wage of a civil servant is 15 dollars a month.
The Cuban government has granted ordinary citizens the right to buy mobile phones just this year; these are mostly used for text messaging, because a 15-minute phone call would cost more than one daily wages.
The use of the internet on the island is extremely limited.
It is available in expensive hotels and for their foreign visitors and on some government connections, such as universities.
Cubans, who often want to apply, have to announce their names to the government.
Access to many websites is prohibited.
Detaining an American in Cuba is a rarity.
Most of a handful of U.S. citizens in prison in Cuba are behind bars for crimes such as drug smuggling, said Gloria Berben, press official at the U.S
Ms Berben said she could not provide more information about the prison.
Imprisonment and detention were clearly amoral.
Such activities, which in any other open society would be legal - let's say free mobile phones - are a criminal offence in Cuba, said Miguel Vivanco, director of the U.S. human rights program, which recently published an unfavorable report on freedoms in Cuba, called "New Castro, the same Cuba," as an allusion to the installation of Raul Castra as the country's leader and a replacement for his sick older brother Fidel.
Vivanco said the defendants are often arrested, tried and imprisoned in one day.
He stated that all solutions would be rather political, and that the Cuban government often provokes a negative reaction in the United States just as these two countries are moving towards more extensive dialogue.
Pakistani government officials are uncovering a conspiracy concerning the sending of men to Afghanistan.
Pakistan authorities on Saturday uncovered an alleged leader of a plot involving the deployment of five men from northern Virginia to Afghanistan to kill members of U.S
Investigators said they were searching for a grey insurgent figure, known as Saifullah, who invited these men to Pakistan after he first discovered them when one of these men posted a commentary approvinging terristic attacks on YouTube's internet video pages.
