Several celebrities participated in the protest, including Danish model and photographer Helena Christensen, who said she had traveled to her mother's birthplace in Peru and provided me with information about the bleak state of the country, in which the influence of climate change that is still taking place.
That's a partial reason why we've decided to join this great march -- going through the world and appealing to the world's leading personalities to present a fair, ambitious and binding agreement, she said.
It is not an easy task, but it is necessary to carry it out, it can not be avoided all the time.
Police prevented protesters from approaching the centre of Bella and said they detained 19 people, primarily because they were either wearing masks or carrying pocket knives.
These activities are prohibited under Danish law during demonstrations.
According to one non-participating spectator who does not wish to be identified because he is involved in climate affairs, activists disguised as athletes and in black suits have placed several charges near the Copenhagen main canal located near several ministries.
They agitated by the buildings, he stated, initiating with flares, but then followed by "several large explosions."
Inside the convention center, people gathered around TV screens to watch the march during the afternoon.
But the protest has probably not penetrated the consciousness of key officials, such as Su Wei, China's chief climate negotiator.
When asked if the demonstration nevertheless had a constructive impact on international negotiations, he replied in English to "Actually, that is something that I was not aware of".
Then he continued Chinese and said, "Since the venue is extensive, I can't hear what's going on outside."
