Concentration of the general public and different sectors and the object of climate change can be seen, he said.
In other words: "It can also be said that this is a violation of the actions or the freedom of other persons.
Test data has revealed constant persistent racial differences
Last week's federal certificate of mathematics knowledge was a welcome element of good news about the public education precinct of Columbia.
Even though the precinct lags far behind the country's high-performance systems, the certificate has showned greater advances among fourth- and eighth-grade pupils over the past two years than in cities including Atlanta, Chicago and New York.
But what remains hidden behind the last numbers of the National Attestation Center for Progress in Teaching are persistent differences in the knowledge achieved between African-Americans and white students, both locally and nationally.
The average result of white pupils in the fourth-grade district of Columbia over the past two years has increased from 267 to 270 points (out of a possible 500), but their African-American peers have grown by only three points, from 209 to 212.
Indeed, the knowledge gap increased between 2007 and 2009 from 53 to 57 points.
Progress among African-Americans in the eighth grade remains essentially balanced, with a diversion of a statistically insignificant one point from 245 to 245.
The average result for white pupils was not included in the test results because the sample size was not large enough.
The picture within six years is not exactly encouraging.
The difference between white and black fourth-grade pupils in 2003, when the first NAEP in the precinct was conducted, was 60 points of the scale (262 to 202).
Although the achieved result in children from both groups improved during this period, the difference was barely closer to 58.
Some advocates of education in the precinct said in the last week that the results, glorified by Secretary Michelle A. Rhee and Mayor Adrian M. Fenty (D), have been more extensively stimulated by white students where there have already been high scores.
This would suggest that we've increased the amount of support for those at the higher end of the scale, which is problematic and disturbing, said Jeff Smith, chief executive of D.C. Voice, a non-profit group that advocates equality of education in the precinct.
I don't jump up and down because of a dispersion of two or three points, D.C. Kwame committee member R. Brown (D-At Large), who has a daughter in the fourth-grade elementary school in Eaton, said.
Frequently criticizing Ms Rhee's style of governance, Ms Brown said the report of test results means secondary education in the city is in a desperate state and requires attention.
It's obvious you always want to see the positives and I respect that.
But what's appalling is that we didn't devote almost any of the time and energy we needed to devote to our secondary education.
Ms. Rhee said the precinct's effort is to continue to look for better ways, focused on the needs of students with low results.
Thus, for example, some teachers are trained in the use of a new reading curricula, Wilson’s reading system, aimed at upper-class elementary school students and high school students who have been unable to master the basics of reading earlier in their cologne career and are significantly lagging behind their peers.
The daily mathematics and sixth-grade mathematics curriculum, emphasizing real-life games and real-life experiences that reached the precinct under former director Clifford Janeyi, can be attributed to NAEP’s progress, which was reported last week.
And I just think that we should continue to work and carry out the best possible intervention in those students who stay below class level, Ms Rhee said.
Others said NAEP's results clarified the question of whether Ms Rhee could continue to increase the overall performance of the system and still provide additional money and resources for low-performance schools, which is necessary to reduce the difference.
It's a certain distinctive dilemma of Ms. Michelle Rhee, said Bruce Fuller, a teaching professor at the University of California, Berkeley, where he studies test results in urban areas.
The conventional remedy procedure is to focus funds and administrative reforms on schools located in the worst parts of the District of Columbia.
In this way, the focus can bring political reverberations directed against an important agenda.
But the complaint also belongs to other city directors, who are attempting to maintain a medium proportion between white and black.
We have to focus on building a system of big schools.
We must strengthen the affinity of our schools so that all families, no matter where they live, are able to rely on their children to be given excellent public instruction.
Mr. Janey, now director of schools in Newark, said in an interview earlier this week that an essential element of reducing the gap between the results achieved is the extension of the traditional public school year, currently 180 days in the precinct, in order to realize the legitimacy of schools.
You have to get up to 200 days in order to enforce the changes, said Mr Janey, who entered into a contract with his association of teachers last year for a 185-day school year, which he considers a "symptom" for looking for a bigger increase next year.
Make higher education part of a better future
The tour bus drove into the Gettysburg track with a loud respiration.
