In fact, one-fifth of this "swollen generation" of Spanish pupils from the dormitory - the remarkable achievements indicate that many of their immigrant parents, mostly Mexicans and residents of Central America, made their way to the United States without finishing college.
Their success story is important, the researchers say, because it underscores their point the way forward for a generation that has played a marginal role in the country's workforce.
Those who have achieved higher education say that they had a natural affection for the school and an innate penetration and effort to succeed.
Many also had parents who set high goals for their children and were looking for ways to replace the ignorance of U.S. education.
But school programs also play an extraordinary role in helping Graciele and the millions of children who would like to go dormitory - especially if these efforts persist over time.
Taking into account that children come from low-income groups whose parents have no experience and the ability to help them navigate the system, any simple intervention at every single point will not solve this, says Patricia Gándara, a researcher at the University of California in Davis, where she studied students from the Latin American field.
We need to think about providing these pre-school-aged interceptions in every way to go through college.
The federal program that funded Graciela's tour on the tracks is a useful example.
Known as GEAR UP, it provides more than US$300 million a year for local school systems to run dormitory preparatory programs that act for students from low-income groups in high schools and continue until those students finish college.
Since 1999, more than 10 million students have served the program, more than 5% of which have entered dormitories, according to U.S. Department of Education data.
Iraqi Ministry of Oil Industry Reaches Deal with 10 Foreign Oil Companies
Notwithstanding fears of violence and political instability, the Iraqi government has managed to get major oil companies to reconstruct their sicking infrastructure during the two auctions that took place on Saturday.
Ten agreements have been reached by Iraq's oil ministry with foreign oil companies, suggesting that Chinese, Russian and European oil companies are ready to play a significant role in restoring Iraq's oil industry, crippled by decades of wars and sanctions.
U.S. companies have left with capital participation in just two out of 10 fields auctioned.
Seven U.S
Only one submitted a lost offer.
Two U.S. companies have reached field agreements that were auctioned in June.
The U.S. oil giant's equal participation in Iraq's open oil industry has surprised analysts.
Iraq finally opened its doors after six years of war, and instead of U.S
This will be for a long time before anything else goes on sale in Iraq.
Concerns about security, highlighted by massive coordinate bombings on Tuesday and political instability, as well as U.S
U.S. firms have been at a disadvantage in some cases, especially Chinese and other government-controlled energy firms have noticeably lower operating costs and are more prone to taking risks because they don't account to shareholders.
Exxon Mobil and Occidental Petroleum Inc. were the only U.S. companies that entered into agreements with the ministry.
Leading U.S. firms, such as Chevron and ConocoPhillips, which have maintained close contacts with Iraq's oil industry and offered technical assistance in recent years have left empty-handed.
Russia's Lukoil and Gazprom companies were top shareholders in two of their contracts, which were awarded at the end of this week.
China's state-run National Petroleum Corp. participated in offers for more contracts than all the other companies and retired with a large stake in contracts for two major fields.
We all know that China strives to become a major economic force, as well as a technological force, oil ministry spokesman Assam Jihad said.
We are convinced that the Chinese company will be comparable to the competition and live up to its commitments to Iraq.
Companies that were preliminarily selected for the submission of tenders, which were then compared by the ministry according to the price per barrel and the ministry is willing to pay to strengthen the output above the current levels in each field.
Al-Qaida's top planner was apparently killed in Pakistan.
In the impact of a U.S. missile near the border between Pakistan and Afghanistan this week, al-Qaida's top-of-the-line planner was apparently killed, according to U.S. anti-terrorism officials on Friday.
As confirmed, this could be the second fatal attack on one of the terrorist's top leaders.
Saleh al-Somali was one of two Arabs who were killed when several missiles landed on their car near Miran Śah in North Vaziristan province, according to U.S. sources and Pakistani officials in the area.
