Local authorities were unable to verify the identities of the victims, but two U.S. counter-terrorism officials cited an unspecified witness who said Somali was dead.
Somali has been cited as a high-ranking planner of al-Qaida's military operations, which has managed the operations of groups of terrorists outside the region of Afghanistan and Pakistan.
He's been involved in screenings around the world, said a senior official who doesn't wish to be named, citing the sensitive nature of U.S. air strikes on Pakistan's territory.
If he held such a central position, he was probably involved in attacks against the United States and Europe.
He received instructions from Al-Qaida's top leadership and, based on them, planned possible terrorist attacks.
Another U.S. official said Somali was rapidly advancing in the al-Qaida hierarchy and was well-connected to other extremist groups in the region.
He didn't have to be a well-known person for some Americans, but he didn't humiliate himself in any way to pose in front of us and our allies, another official said.
