He said that "a package of incentives and inhibitions" would require "it would prompt the Iranian government to actually feel less secure with nuclear weapons" because "its residents will suffer extraordinarily" from sanctions.
According to Friday's opinion issued by White House spokeswoman Robert Gibbs, the administration engaged European leaders in a warning that Iran would have serious consequences if it did not put its nuclear program into full compliance with the U.N. nuclear security committee and its supervisory body, the International Atomic Energy Agency.
Iran insists that it wants to develop its expertise only for peaceful purposes.
