In aging China, a change of course
Wang Weijia and her husband grew up surrounded by propaganda posters on which they read that "Mother Earth is too exhausted to feed more children" and "One extra child means one extra grave."
Then they learned a lesson that when official government sites in Shanghai, alarmed by the low birth rate and aging population in their city, they suddenly changed course this summer and began encouraging young couples, their reaction was immediate and determined: Never.
We have always devoted all our time and energy to one child.
We left nothing for others, says Wang, 31, a human resources manager who has an 8-month-old son.
China's more than 30-year-old policy of having only one child has been established, creating two generations of only notoriously chubby and pampered children, affectionately nicknamed "little emperors," the country is threatened by a population crisis.
The average birth rate has fallen to 1.8 children per pair compared to six, compared to six compared to the policy, according to U.N. data.
Dividing the population, where the population, aged 60 and above, is predicted tormentously to grow from 16.7 percent of the population in 2020 to 31.1 percent in 2050. That's far above an overall average of around 20 percent.
