Last year, people aged 60 and above accounted for nearly 22 percent of Shanghai's registered population, while the birth rate was smaller than one child per couple.
Xie Lingli, director of Shanghai's municipal population and family planning commission, said many couples must have children to "help reduce the proportions of an aging population and reduce the numerical status of the workforce in the future."
He says it's about "being old doesn't mean being rich -- like in evolved countries such as Japan or Sweden."
