As it was inserted into the country's constitution in 1978, China's one-child policy is arguably the most controversial mandate established under Communist Party rule to this day.
The pair, which violated politics, faced immense fines -- up to three times their annual salary in some areas -- and discrimination at work.
China's official sites have championed policies that have helped prevent the country from drawing on natural resources, while human rights defenders have been condemned in pursuing policies.
In rural areas, some officials forced pregnant women on a second child to undergo abortions.
In addition, many couples had to undergo sexist-special abortions, leading to an unnaturally high ratio of men towards women.
In past years, population officials have been progressively milder in their opinion on one-chief policy.
In 2004, as a rule, they allowed exemptions - including residents of cities, members of national minorities, and cases where male and female were only children -- and in 2007 alleviated the dictions of their slogans promoting a hard line.
Qiao Xiaochun, a professor at the University of Beijing's Institute of Population Research, said central government officials had previously debated more radical changes, like allowing couples to have two children if one of the partners was an only child.
In June, Shanghai became China's first city to launch an aggressive campaign to incite multiple births.
Almost overnight, posters, urging parents to have just one child, were replaced with copies of the regulation, describing in detail how it would be justified to have a second child and how to apply for permission.
The city administration sent family planning officials and volunteers to meet couples in their homes and insert leaflets under the door.
They also promised to provide emotional and financial advice for those who decided to have more than one child.
The reaction was cold, family planning officials said.
Grieving response
Although officials in one rural city around Shanghai have seen an increase in interest among couples who would like to have a second child after launching a campaign, most urban precincts did not report changes.
Huinan's urban precinct with a population of 115000, for example, still registered four to five cases per month.
Disappointed officials in Shanghai said that despite the campaign, the number of births in the city in 2010 is still expected only around 16,5000 - slightly higher than in 2009, but lower than in 2008.
Feng Juying, head of a family planning committee in Shanghai's Caol district, said financial considerations were probably the main reason why people don't want to have more children.
They want to provide the best for their first child, he said.
Yang Jiawei, 27, and his wife, Liu Juanjuan, 26, said she would like to have two children and have been legally allowed to do so.
But like many Chinese who have only inadequate health and life insurance provided by the government.
Without a docial net, the choice could be irresponsible.
People in the west assess one-child politics poorly as a contentious legal issue, says Yang, a civil engineer whose wife is seven months pregnant in union with her first child.
Yes, we've been deprived of the chance to have more than one child.
But the problem doesn't just lie in politics.
It's about money.
Other couples cite psychological reasons for hesitation.
Wang, human resources manager, says they only want one child because she was also an only child: "We were the focus of our families' attention, and they all cared for us.
We weren't used to provide care and we didn't really want to take care of others.
Chen Zijian, the 42-year-old who owns a translation company, expressed this much more openly.
For middle-class two-career parents who have reduced birth rates, he said, being successful means being selfish.
Today's 20 and 30-year-old adults view parents as permeating the first days of the Chinese experiment with capitalism and do not want this way of nourishment for themselves, he says.
It's just one child that puts enormous demands on parents' time, he says.
The mother must sacrifice at least two years of her social life.
Then there are still contentious questions of the propstor -- "You have to rebuild your apartment" -- and strategies -- "You have to have a summary ready at a time when the child is 9 months old for a good pre-school upbringing.
Most of his friends want to settle for one child, Che says, but not with two.
We're the first generation with higher living standards, he says.
We don't want to bring too many sacrifices.
The U.N. group outlined a plan to reduce emissions.
The U.N.-sponsored climate conference -- characterised by far-reaching undisciplined stances and mutual misleading -- once again on Friday focused on easing a document proposing an ambitious reduction in the amount of greenhouse gases over the next 40 years with industrialized nations bearing most of the burden as soon as possible.
The text, which can provide a starting point for a final political agreement on the regulation of greenhouse gases, highlights the remaining obstacles as well as illuminates the way forward.
But important progress has been seen in negotiations that took place at the wrong time with more than 100 world leaders arriving in Copenhagen next week.
Created by the U.N. ad hoc working group, this text inconspicuously states how much money rich countries should provide to those poor for dealing with global warming in the short and long term.
And it provides an entire range of choices for key issues, including how to develop and cope with emerging economies in order to curb their carbon output, and what should be the upper limit of the rise in the global temperature that political officials will still be able to tolerate.
This will add a great deal of flexibility to the process, says John Coequyt, chief representative of the Washington-based club Sierra.
Michael Zammit Cutajar, who proposed a six-page document, refreshed from 180 pages of the text from a meeting to focus on what a top climate official at the U.N., Yvo de Boer, described as a "great picture."
It outlines the outlines for a possible deal in which industrial nations could jointly limit their emissions to 25 to 45 percent by year compared to 1990 levels, while major developing countries could reduce their emissions during that period to 15 to 30 percent.
Together, the country would reduce emissions between 50 and 95 percent by 2050.
The European Union urgently debated a promise on Friday that it would provide $3.6 trillion over the next three years to help poorer countries adopt adaptations to climate change -- from managing floods and droughts to preventing deforestation.
But he still released Friday the same kind of verbal fireworks that prevailed in last week's interviews.
U.S. U.S. Special Envoy on Climate Issues Todd Stern dismissed a speech calling for binding restrictions on greenhouse gas emissions for industrialized countries compared to volunteering among major emerging economies if they were not funded by the developed world.
The move signaled that the Obama administration established a tougher line against China than Bush administration officials just two years ago.
The United States will not accede to the treaty without transferring major developing countries and carrying out actions, Stern, who has also complained that the text is not sufficient to secure restrictions and should be screened by outside observers.
Stern made that comment an hour after China's Deputy Secretary of State He Yafei told U.S. chief negotiators on climate issues that either a lack of "common sense" or "extraordinary irresponsibility" led earlier in the week to communicate that the United States would not help China financially in order to deal with global warming.
With the future of the world's main powers at stake in the economic runway of the world's major powers, tug-of-war has erupted both within the developing world and between the industrial world and emerging economies.
The current struggle is mostly about preserving individual economies rather than maintaining the planet, between China and the United States to fulfill their respective commitments, while poorer nations point out that two dozen of the world's most influential countries ignore the scientific imperatives to carry out stronger actions.
Ricardo Ulate, a Costaria delegate, said it was not surprising that major powers were fighting over who should bear the cost of limiting greenhouse gases, while vulnerable countries have become much more aggressive in finding a way to prevent large issuers responsibly in their actions.
It's clearly a game that new economic hegemony is unfolding, said Ulate, who also serves as a regional adviser on regional climate change in Mexico and Central America for international nature conservation.
Some of the countries most vulnerable to climate change have indicated that they would like to pursue a legally binding deal in Copenhagen, but most of the main participants said the best outcome of the talks would be a political deal.
An alliance of small island states, which has 43 members, created a 24-page draft deal last Friday morning.
Artur Runge-Metzger, who led international climate negotiations in favour of the European Commission, said that enforcement by small island nations had "evoked political pressure on the entire political process" partly because they are now united and demanding actions from growing economies such as China and India.
Negotiations have prompted a new urgency as soon as delegates have focused on the fact that they have to resolve most of the remaining contentious issues before the heads of government meet for an agreement.
High-ranking officials, such as Indian Environment Minister Jairam Ramesh and China's deputy minister, pulled out of planes and ran through the halls of the Bella center to the closed doors of meetings and new conferences so they were able to set claims that will be assessed next week.
The utter vastness of the assembly - when 13000 people were moved daily from and into the convention center, guitar-playing activists staged at night the ironic spectacles about the countries they thought had sent, and there was a hand-in-hand-to-handed proposal on paper rather than by e-mail -- controlled by a challenge.
Intensity is only a superstructure: in Copenhagen, almost almost all key ministers were present, and before Wednesday, 60 heads of state.
We've gotten into high categories, Carlos Manuel Rodriguez said, Deputy Minister for Global Policy on International Conservation.
The heavy weights are coming.
Britain's first train with a speed of 140 miles per hour redefined feelings this Monday
At 5:13 a.m. on Monday morning, the whistle cut through the darkness as a sign of the train's departure, which marked a transport revolution in Britain.
The country's first high-speed passenger train reaches a speed of 140 miles per hour and flies from Ashford in Kent to London's St Pancras and passes a distance of 58 miles in 38 minutes.
Lord Adonis, Minister of Transport, then joined Ms. Kelly Holmes and boarded his first high-speed passenger kit outside of London.
He hopes the new service will eventually connect Britain with an international network of fast, reliable trains and will combine large agglomerations of Midland, the north and Scotland with London.
Neglected standing waters should shorten transport times in passenger transport and on business trips, and switching from aircraft and cars to rail could reduce carbon emissions and eliminate congestion on the roads.
In theory, three major political parties have been acquired for this vision.
But dozens of trills of pounds, necessary to build a new national rail network, the inevitable fight over projects and concerns about environmental disruption have now hampered the construction of Britain's high-speed lines.
High Speed Two (HS2), a company, created in January to provide the government with a feasible study, forwarded its report to Lord Adonis on December 30, as reported by the Times.
It provided him with a detailed map of the routes of the next planned stage of the high-speed network.
The new tracing of London's connection with western Midland has been designed within five metres in urban areas and neighbourhoods, where impacting the environment could be problematic.
In the open countryside, the final route was planned.
HS2 also foresees setting three options for a large-scale high-speed network leading north.
Lord Adonis responds in the spring.
The preferred option is a Y-shaped confibulation, when one high-speed track should lead to western Midland.
The track should divide near Birmingham with one branch, passing from Pennines to Manchester and Scotland and the other, leading northeast to Sheffield, Leeds and Newcastle over Tyne.
One track should lead to Scotland.
"It looks like there's been the most powerful choice," he said, and a railway expert.
As soon as it's done, the journey times between London and Edinburgh are reduced to two hours and 40 minutes.
The first stage, which won't be open before 2025, could allow passengers to travel between London and Birmingham in 49 minutes compared to more than one hour in the present.
Proposals should allow trains to run at 2250 miles per hour, which would make the UK's network the fastest in Europe.
The initial route is already designed to carry trains of 400 m in length, which would be able to carry 1100 people.
Up to 18 rigs could operate every hour on the London-Birming track.
This means that the London terminal would be able to serve up to 20000 passengers per hour if necessary.
Due to capital constraints, it is projected that an existing station would be expanded in such a way as taking a high-speed network.
Experts from the railway industry say that only San Pancras International or Euston offer such potential.
Lord Adonis announces whether or not he intends to implement this scheme, but large-scale public consultations and planning procedures mean that what the final decision will not take place before the general election.
Even if the wheels move, the net should be built in parts.
Trains should first be put into operation not on high-speed lines, but pre-existing lines north of Birmingham, and maintain journey times between London and Scotland for more than three hours.
This is regarded as a crucial point of contact to ensure the transition from aircraft to trains, a landmark for the rail revolution.
Taxpayers reimburse mortgage of 75000 pounds for Tory's orangery
Alan Duncan, holders of the Tory's leading seat bench in the Chamber of Deputies, were paid thousands of pounds by taxpayers last year for an orangery he had built at his home in the constituency.
The party's spokesperson for the prison, who was degraded by David Cameron for complaining that MPs lived "out of allocations," raised his mortgage to 75000 pounds to build an oak-lined extension at his second house in Rutland.
He was allowed to charge a special interest, hundreds of pounds in a month to taxpayers.
The municipal authorities approved the claims in a timely manner and so it was not interrogated by Sir Thomasen Legg, who carried out the control of the member's expenses. Last night, Mr. Duncan made his demands "it should not have been cleaner or simpler."
Mr. Duncan, one of Parliament's wealthiest members, owns two two-storey houses in the village in his constituency of Rutland and Melton.
Houses are worth approximately a million pounds.
There's a kitchen, a living room and a dining room downstairs, but the tax office agreed that Mr. Duncan needed more space.
Last year he added a winter garden to the house, which is in the plans marked as an "orangery."
Neighbors describe it as "a greenhouse for fun and treats."
Mr. Duncan was not asked to provide any explanation for the increased loan during the last inspection by the Committee on Municipal Standards and Privileges in the latest mortgage settlement.
The committee told him last month that he had violated the rules, after reviewing his spending.
In 2004, Mr. Duncan changed pupillary security to a mortgage of 271406 pounds from his property in London to a house in his constituency, which he bought in 1991, a year before he became a member of the House of Commons.
The documents released this week showed that Mr Duncan claimed approximately 1400 pounds a month in the interest of a mortgage until March last year.
In April, its demands increased to more than 1800 pounds per month.
Mr Duncan's RBS mortgage repayment interest couldn't change at this time, suggesting that all of this was intended to finance a loan of 75000 pounds.
The standard RBS mortgage repayment variable declined from 7.94 percent in December 2007 to 4 percent in March of this year, while remaining.
The last document of the municipal authorities shows that Mr. Duncan continued claiming 1250 pounds per month this May.
Mr. Duncan left his job as a shadowy head of the House in September after one secret newspaper filmed him and said deputies had to survive "on allocation" following the spending scandal.
"Basically, it's nationalized, you have to live on the ratio and you're being treated like a s**t."
He said: "I've spent money on my garden and entitlement and a small fraction for what was needed.
And I could claim the whole damn share, but I won't."
The MP, a millionaire from his former career as an oil trader, was first invited to explain his editions in May when it became clear that he claimed thousands of pounds for his garden, even before approval by the tax authority that it "may be considered excessive."
One of the activists dug a pound emblem-shaped pit in Mr. Duncan's turf in protest after revealing he had claimed 4000 pounds for three years.
Speaking about a mortgage increase last night, Mr Duncan said: "It was because of the capital increase, which was approved by both the tax office and subsequently Legg."
He added: "this is not a contentious issue of hiding and zigzagging or breaking rules or anything else.
It's an absolutely straight line down, according to the rules, justified and that's it.
It couldn’t have been cleaner or simpler in many other ways.”
Come and take a close look at the cottage on the Brighton coast with the advent calendar.
Coasural cottages in Brighton have found a new meaning of living in the winter months as an interactive advent calendar.
Beyond, an alternative ecclesiastical grouse that embolishes climation by creativity urged 24 owners to decorate their cottages for every day in December, and to use Christmas carols as the theme of this decoration.
The event began on December 1, when it allowed visitors to view the last cottage from 17.30 to 18.30 every day, and bishop's sandwich and mulled wine, provided free of charge.
The selected carols included I Saw Three Ships, O Come, O Come, Emmanuel and The First Noël, where the chalet represented the work of Janette Tozera, a local artist.
Martin Poole, 50, an assistant professor at Hove's television marketing, is a non-scholard priest in the Chiechester diocese and head of Beyond.
He said: "We want to make religion much more important to people in post-Christian society.
"Why do we, as a church, expect people to come into a strange old building? I feel that the church should come to the people and serve in a waking manner.
"The idea of an advent calendar in cottages originated just as an interview with some friends during dinner.
Brighton is such a fantastically creative and throbbing place and we have tried to try and present it in a spiritual way.”
Strong mobilization in Copenhagen and the world against climate change.
Tens of thousands of people took to the streets on Saturday demanding a strong and binding agreement to fight global warming, which should be adopted at a conference in Copenhagen, demonstrated around the world as well as in Copenhagen, where police arrested hundreds of people on that occasion.
At least thirty thousand people, according to police sources and one hundred thousand, according to organizers, demonstrated in cold weather in the Danish capital on the occasion of the U.N. climate conference, which aims to adopt by Friday an agreement that could come into force on January 1, 2013.
Connie Hedegaard, the Danish president of the climate conference, said the growing global mobilization, the clear evidence of which is current demonstrations, has contributed to the fact that the "political tax" of a possible failure in Copenhagen would be too high.
According to an AFP journalist, there were incidents in the Danish capital shortly after the opening of the procession, when a group of about 300 protesters, dressed in black, began smashing shop windows with cobblestones and hammers.
The riot police intervened emphatically immediately.
In total, police announced the arrests of between 600 and 700 people, specifying that they were mainly members of the "Blacks Blocks", ultraradical separate groups, which had already manifested in April at the NATO summit in Strasbourg, in eastern France.
The Climate Justice Action (CJA), one of the organizers of the event, condemned the conditions under which hundreds of radicals were arrested "indiscriminately" and stressed that about a hundred of them were handcuffeded and seated "held in the streets on Saturday evening" despite the extreme winter.
After leaving Parliament, the procession calmly made its way in the direction of the Bella Center, where negotiations were taking place.
The protesters stopped about 500 meters from the building without trying to penetrate inside.
For the speakers, a podium was erected, in front of which a clergy meeting by candles took place with the participation of former South African Archbishop of Cape Town Desmond Tutu.
Most of the protesters arrived by bus and train from major European cities, and in addition to Africans, there were also a large number of participants from Asia, including several Chinese and Koreans.
Nearly 3,000 people, dressed mostly in blue raincoats, formed their first rally in Copenhagen in the morning at the call of Friends of the Earth, which wanted to create "blue waves" for a "fair climate."
We're taking to the streets today to demand a settlement of the environmental debt in favor of the south, explained Lidy Nacpil, a Filipino fighter from the Jubilee South Coalition in Copenhagen.
We can't say anymore: it's time for that, Benin singer Angelique Kidjo said.
In Africa, rivers and watercourses dry up, today we can walk in them, and this has never been possible before.
For the first time in the history of climate diplomacy, which was born in 1992 with the adoption of the United Nations Convention, the movement to fight for another world approached environmental organizations.
French MEP José Bová, one of the figures fighting for another world, said he had come to Copenhagen with the aim of "connecting climate and social justice." "There is currently no difference between the fight against global warming and the fight for another world."
The first demonstrations began in the Asian-Pacific region, in which there are many islands particularly sensitive to climate warming.
About 50,000 people took to the streets in Australia, according to organizers.
In Manila, several hundred people marched, mostly students, in red clothes and with scarves evoking the importance of solar energy.
In Hong Kong, Jakarta, but also in Canada, several hundred protesters took to the streets with the demand for strong action against climate change.
Hundreds of people took part in protests organized by the "350" movement in France, primarily in Paris, Marseille (south), Lille (north), Bordeaux (West) and Lyon (Eastern).
Several dozen people gathered in Geneva.
Separatist Abkhazia has elected its president, Georgia condemns it as a "farce"
Abkhazia elected its president on Saturday, about a year after Moscow recognized this pro-Russian separatist territory as independent from Georgia, Tbilisi, by contrast, declares the move a "farce."
About 131,000 people were invited to participate in these first elections after the Russian-Georgian war in August 2008 for control of South Ossetia, another former Georgian territory, recognized by Russia.
Polling stations closed at 17pm GMT.
According to officials of the Abkhazi electoral commission, the turnout was 58% a few hours before polls closed.
The first preliminary results are expected to be announced at night from Saturday to Sunday.
All five candidates, including incumbent President Sergei Bagapsha, categorically reject the idea of merging with Georgia, which condemned this election.
This election is a farce.
On the other hand, Georgian President Mikhail Saakashvili "regrets that Abkhazia has completely fallen into Russian rule," its spokeswoman, Manana Manjgaladze, said.
In addition to Russia, only Nicaragua and Venezuela have recognized the independence of the territory with 216,000 inhabitants, while the rest of the world considers it part of Georgia, which has been illegally occupied by Russian troops.
Following separation from Georgia, after the 1992-1993 conflict in which several thousand people died, Abkhazia is trying to show through elections that it is ready to return to international politics.
This process is one of the stages of our new life, a new era of our independent state, Kvarčija Svetlanová, a 54-year-old historian, told AFP.
She was one of those who came to vote at a polling post at a school in the capital Suchumi, into a beautiful white building with several palm trees at the entrance.
The question of Russian domination is the main theme of presidential elections in this region on the shores of the Black Sea, which can pride itself on beautiful nature.
Throughout this small country with 216,000 inhabitants, huge billboards are deployed with Bagapsh alongside Russian President Dmitry Medvedev and Russian Prime Minister Vladimir Putin.
In Gaul, in the west of the country, Russian officials, who had come to oversee the successful conduct of the elections, received a warm reception by the district mayor of Beslan Arshba.
"We Abkhazians are grateful "said at a small ceremony, referring to the support of Moscow and, in particular, its decision to recognize Abkhazia's independence.
I hope that your country will soon be recognized by the United Nations, said Alexei Ostrovsky, a member of the Russian parliament and toasted a glass of vodka on it.
The opposition, which was represented in the elections by former vice-president Raul Khadzhimba and two traders Zaus Ardzinba and Beslan Boutba, criticised the treaties concluded last year with Moscow, such as a treaty that allows Russia control of Abkhazia's rail network for 10 years.
A fifth-ranked candidate, Vitali Bganba, says he doesn't support either the current government or the opposition.
To win elections, an absolute majority of 50% plus one vote is required.
If none of the candidates achieves this result, the decisive fight must take place within two weeks.
