Geogring and history subjects will be strengthened as part of the general grammar school's reform, Chatel said.
The Minister of Education Luc Chatel said on Sunday during an interview with JDD magazine that the subjects of history and geography will be "strengthened" within the framework of the reform of general grammar schools, even though their teaching will no longer be mandatory in the final year of grammar school with a focus on exact sciences.
Now all second-year students will have four hours a week, and for everyone there will be the same program from general culture, Chatel confirmed.
According to the minister, "it is necessary to end hypocrisy" because, according to him, specialization S is no longer focused on exact sciences" but "basically the best students are in it".
The specialization of ES and L cannot be sub-specializations, continues and emphasizes: "I have not heard many historians and intellectuals who would complain that geography and history are not taught in the last year of professional grammar schools".
The Ifop poll, published on Saturday by the Humanité newspaper, showed that seven French out of ten (69%) disagreed with the government's project to abolish compulsory history and geography teaching in the senior year with specialization S, which is why many intellectuals and teachers criticised this cancellation, both the left and some MPs of the right.
On the contrary, the minister added that "it is not possible" to abolish the philosophy in the program of specialization With the Graduation Year.
At the same time, Luc Chatel defended the reform in the education of teachers, while 16 organizations (students and unions) called for participation in Tuesday's mobilization against this project, which, according to them, sacrifices the pedagogical education of future grammar school professors.
As part of Master's Degree 2 (M2) at the beginning of the year, he will undergo an entrance exam focused on their field and at the end of the year to verify their ability to teach, the minister explained.
They will participate in professional internships during the second year of the master's degree and will perform in front of the pupils as a professor-intern.
If you add up all the internships that will be offered to you prior to the admission procedure, you will gain more experience with pupils, nearly 300 hours, continued .
The minister also announced that he would propose a "personal right to education," which does not yet exist in national education.
He reaffirmed a project to scrap the school's map by 2012.
At the elite École normale supérieure university, experts have 15 minutes to convince about the climate.
Fifteen minutes to present the objectives of the Copenhagen summit: this is a challenge announced by Jean Jouzel, multi-president of the Intergovernmental Group of Experts on Climate Development (GIEC) at the initiative of the University of the Ecole normale supérieure on Ulm Street.
According to one AFP journalist, Jean Jouzel opened the laureate of the Nobel Peace Prize "Ernest", which is a kind of one-day seminar composed of fifteen-minute contributions by experts that will be filmed and open to the public.
We wanted to do something that would attract people and on the basis of which they would have a greater desire to learn something more, explained 22-year-old Edouard Hannezo, one of the organizers of this seminar, who was named after a fish from a local pond, which was named by students.
About twenties of contributing experts from individual scientific disciplines will be selected and specified that the selection is based on their charisma.
“We can talk for hours about climate warming, but we can also summarize the basic goals in a few minutes,” Jean Jouzel told AFP, who spoke in front of fifty listeners without pre-prepared notes, only using graphs.
It's part of our scientific work, he emphasized with the hope that listeners will recognize that "the Copenhagen conference relies on very seriously prepared scientific foundations."
The videos filmed at these lectures will be placed on the school's website from December 15.
The next "Ernest" seminar is planned for February, and others will follow with regularity every two months.
Delays in payments: angry guards in prisons, planned measures
On Friday, prison warden unions announced a "general mobilization" as they demand that premiums and overtime be reimbursed until the Christmas holidays, the payment of which has been postponed to January for financial reasons.
Prisonal FO (the 2nd departments of the guards) asked employees who will not be on duty to take part in a demonstration taking place on Tuesday morning in front of prison facilities, and to "block" them, drawing attention to the "theft of their wages."
The CGT has called on them to "convene gatherings in individual prisons and launch a major mobilization", saying it is "disgraceful and unacceptable" that "overtime and surcharges for working at night, on Sundays and on holidays" worked in October would not be paid in December.
The first unions of guards, the Federal Union of Prison Wardens (Ufap/Unsa), "calls for total mobilization (...) in order to show dissatisfaction and indignation."
The administrative directorates and the Ministry of Justice explain that the number of overtimes increased a lot in 2009 and were not all included in the financial budget, but assure that the amounts owed will be paid in January.
Chancellorship said at the end of the day that Michèle Alliot-Mari had sent a message to the unions of prison guards assuring them that the payment would be made next month.
I know of the efforts made by prison staff to ensure the functioning and modernisation of our prison system, the Minister writes in her communication.
I have ordered the administrative directorate of prisons to take immediate measures so that the situation, which arose from a poor evaluation of the number of overtime, will never be repeated in the future, Minister Alliot-Mari added.
The December wage will therefore be low, with the exception of executives who are given the famous premiums for the performance of their duties and the achievement of objectives, Ufap/Unsa states, and for those "there is always a place in the budget".
It's simply a scandal, outraged the FO interregional union in Marseille, announcing its decision to boycott "all meetings and summonses" until further notice.
Workers will not come to work on Sundays or holidays, if they are not reimbursed for these days of October in December, as well as overtime, there is also a risk of FO Rhône-Alpes-Auvergne.
At a time when more and more work is being given to us without being given additional staff, it is sad to allow the lowest-ranking employees to pay for it, according to the CGT-prison wardens in Bordeaux.
According to trade unions, the situation is not the same in all regions, some are better off than others.
However, in any case, there are few funds, said the Secretary-General of Ufap, Jean-François Forget.
Obama says he gives "better gifts" at Christmas than the ones he receives
U.S. President Barack Obama, who is preparing for his first christmas with his family at the White House, confided "goddess" of Oprah Winfrey's screen that in terms of gifts, he has a simple rule: "I give better gifts than the ones I receive."
The president and his wife, Michelle, spoke in a special edition of the show, which will be aired on ABC on Sunday night about how they are going to spend the Christmas holidays and the end of the year in their new and prestigious residence.
Even Bo, a small four-legged companion of the family, gets his gift.
Jesus also likes Boa, Michelle Obama said, adding that the dog will also "of course" receive a gift, according to excerpts from the show's preliminary broadcast.
And Michelle Obama also objected to her man's claims about the quality of the gifts she's getting: 'I'm sure it's not, I gave you prima gifts last year,' she teased the president.
Well, that's true, of course the president was forced to agree, but immediately afterwards, with humor, he pointed his finger at the first lady's pearl necklace: "And who gave you this?"
On this one-day very relaxed show with the famous presenter, which fiercely supported Barack Obama in his presidential campaign, the Obamas also talked about their favorite donors they received as children.
Baralack Obama introduced a bike with a ten-degree transmission, as well as the basketball that his Kenyan father gave to him for Christmas, which he encountered very rarely.
I remember when I saw my father, who came to see us at Christmas and how he gave me that basketball, the president said.
It wasn't until long afterwards that I realised he'd given me the ball.
The first lady, in turn, remembered the dollhouse.
I didn't even know how to organize it all in the house, so I balanced all the furniture along the wall instead of placing it around the fireplace, but I loved that little dollhouse, Michelle Obam reminisced.
Johnny Hallyday re-operated, RTL reports he's in artificial sleep
Johnny Hallyday has been re-operated in Los Angeles because of "injuries" developed after a major operation he has undergone in France, but the situation, according to his loved ones, is "under control," while RTL reported that the singer is in artificial sleep to prevent pain and complications.
The 66-year-old rocker was re-operated on the night from Wednesday to Thursday at Cedars-Sinai Hospital in Los Angeles after U.S. doctors noted "injuries resulting from the operation" of the prolapsed disc, which he underwent in Paris on November 26, his press department said in Paris, adding that these lesions required "repair surgery."
However, the singer's press department tried to reassure the public and said the situation was "under control" while announcing that a new medical communiqué would be released within 48 hours at the latest.
According to RTL, which is the lead partner of the singer's latest tour, Johnny Hallyday has been put into artificial sleep to prevent "pain and complications."
In 48 hours, when the patient opens his eyes, we'll be a little more confident, RTL adds.
The press department did not confirm or commentate this information when asked by AFP.
The RTL also reports that, according to "some sources," there was no drainer stationed after the first operation in Paris.
Rocker was operated on at Parc Monceau's international clinic because of a prolapsed disc.
The operation, which had already been scheduled several days in advance, was performed by neurosurgeon Stephan Delajoux, a physician well known in artistic circles.
However, the doctor was not available on Thursday to comment on RTL information.
After that surgery, singer Johnny Hallyday was hospitalized Monday at Cedars-Sinai Hospital in Los Angeles because of an infection that occurred after the intervention.
Singer Eddy Mitchell, a close friend of Johnny Hallydaye, told RTL radio on Thursday that his friend was going through "the wrong period," but added that he "don't worry" about the rocker because he's a big "fighter."
He called me just before flying to Los Angeles, but I told him he's doing stupid things because spending 12 hours on a plane after such surgery is no fun, Eddy Mitchell said.
On Wednesday night, the singer's producer Jean-Claude Camus reassured the situation, saying Johnny Hallyday "responds well to antibiotics" and that the infection has been "stopped."
Mr Camus also said that he had met Laeticia, the singer's wife, and that he had not noticed any special concerns.
The singer's health problems have only multiplied since July, when he was hospitalized for nine days after falling on his yacht.
Two months later, to everyone's surprise, it was revealed that Johnny Hallyday had been operated on "small tumor of the small intestine" and then on a weak infection.
At the end of September, the singer returned to his long "Tower 66" tour, which is presented as his farewell.
But since then, every one of his health issues has been very monitored and commented. Fans and journalists have noticed that the singer has difficulty moving on stage.
In mid-November, his team again had to deny the news of a new hospitalization.
Euro-2009/small pool: Prime Minister Bousquet in his favorite discipline
Frender Frédérick Bousquet, a three-time medalist at the 2009 World Championships in a large swimming pool, won the title at the 2009 European Championships in a small swimming pool in a discipline he particularly likes: the 50m freestyle.
On the first day of the championship, on which overalls are allowed for the last time, only three world records fell, although much more were expected.
One of them was surpassed by Hungarian racer Evelyn Verrasztova at the 200 m position race and the two remaining men's relay of Russia on a 4x50 m position race.
Great grief for Bousquet, who was interested in a world record of 50 m in a free way, of which he is already a holder in a large swimming pool (20.94).
In one of the two royal disciplines of swimming, the 50m freestyle, the 28-year-old Frenchman won with a final time of 20.53 seconds in the final, at which ten swimmers kicked off instead of the usual eight.
Bousquet defeated Croat Duje Draganja (20.70) and Russian Sergei Fesikov (20.84), but failed to break the world record (20.30), as he anticipated.
We can always dream of something better, but I've said if there's going to be a world record in my range, I'm going to try for it.
Like Bousquet, almost all swimmers wore jumpsuits made of polyurethane, which will be banned from January 1.
The overalls have helped 238 world records since February 2008 and their era ends in Istanbul, which first hosted them at the same competition in 1999.
The use of the jumpsuit this time was not helped even by the German Paul Biedermann, the two-time world champion in the big pool in the summer in Rome, who had a world record at his fingertips.
The young swimmer failed to break the 400-meter freestyle (3:32.77), but still won with the second-fastest time of his career (3:34.55), in front of Russian racer Nikita Lobintsev (3:35.75) and Dane Mads Glaesner (3:36.82)
I didn't put everything into the race.
I knew I wouldn't break the world record this time, but I managed to defend my title and I accomplished my task.
Lobintsev will be a serious opponent for the future, Biedermann explained.
U.S. record of Romanesque sarcophagus belonging to Zola has been auctioned for US$1.5 million
A record of the Romanesque sarcophagus, which belonged to none other than French writer Emil Zola, was auctioned for US$1.5 million on Thursday, significantly more that was estimated, the art dealer Sotheby's said.
The price of this two-metre-long and 63-cm high headboard, dating from the third century AD, was estimated at 150,000 to 250,000 dollars.
Mramor "representing four dinosaur scenes in sophisticated architectural decor, surrounded by satyrs and bakchants is a rare work, there are very few similar pieces in the world, perhaps four or five," Florent Heintz, Vice-President of Ancient Roman and Egyptian Art at Sotheba's, told AFP on Tuesday.
The work was sold among a hundred others.
The sale is still ongoing, but it's an item that's probably been bid on the most, Sotheby's spokesperson, Dan Abernethy, told AFP.
That Emile Zola bought this marble was discovered a few days ago.
The album had been in Rome for almost 300 years in the famous collection of the Borgia family, and then gradually appeared with French actress Cecile Sorel, who adorned a bathtub with her in her private hotel in Paris and with Paul Reynaud, the Minister of the Third Republic in France, and briefly the Chairman of the Council before coming to the power of Marshal Petain.
She remained shaded there for about fifty years.
Florent Heintz discovered during a search of the museum's database in the Louvre that this board had been part of "Emil Zola's "remnants" opened since 1903, a year after the death of author "Therese Raquin" and the fresco "Rougon-Macquart."
Intermittent attempt to exit President Zelaya from Honduras to Mexico.
Mexico on Thursday concluded that the conditions for the transport of the deposed president of Honduras were not met, Manuel Zelaya, to whom the rebels had refused to give a guarantee of a safe exit.
After the telephone conversation I had with President Zelaya last night, everything indicates for the time being that there is no way to carry out this transfer, Mexican Foreign Minister Patricia Espinos said.
Mexico's governmental plane, which flew to El Salvador for President Zelayau again on Wednesday, said a source secretary-general for migration.
The coups government wanted to force President Zelaya to "resign" and to apply for political asylum abroad, only in this way it would give him the possibility of a safe exit.
But the deposed president rejected those demands because he wanted to go to Mexico as exiled head of state.
"The coup government "has experienced yet another setback when it wanted me to give up my office (...), wanting me to resign," Zelaya told Globo radio on Thursday, often commenting on the political crisis, which has been going on in this Central American country for five months.
I can stay here for ten years, I've got my guitar here, the overthrown president, hided at the Brazilian embassy in Tegucigalpa for two and a half months and played a few chords.
The Embassy of Mexico has asked us to provide a guarantee for safe transport, but unfortunately we cannot accept it because it does not include an application for asylum, the interior minister of the coup government, Oscar Raul Matute told Honduran HRN radio on Wednesday.
I won't seek asylum for any country, Manuel Zelaya said in an interview with Telesur, which is headquartered in Caracas.
If I ever leave Honduras, I will leave as president of the Honduran people, he stressed.
Rumours of Zelaya's departure mobilised supporters of the deposed president on Wednesday, who came to gather around Brazil's embassy, where the army strengthened protection.
Brazil's foreign minister, Celso Amorim, has criticized the attitude of the coup government.
They show their marginal stance on international norms, he added.
Zelaya, who bonded with Socialist Venezuelan President Hugo Chávez, was overthrown on June 28, the day he organized in preparation for his re-election public consultation against the recommendations of the Supreme Court, the Army and Congress.
He unexpectedly returned from exile in Costaria on September 21 and found refuge in the Brazilian embassy.
If he comes out of there, he will be arrested and held accountable for the crime of "high treason" from which he is accused, according to the coup government.
On November 29, coup president Roberto Micheletti managed to organize a presidential election to replace President Zelay.
Porfirio Lobo won controversial elections, recognized by the United States, but condemned by Latin American countries such as Venezuela, Argentina and Brazil.
Three days later, Congress voted against President Zelaya's return to power until the end of his mandate on January 27.
Wall Street rises, U.S
The New York Stock Exchange ended with a rise on Thursday, and U.S. trade balance results indicate a rise in both the United States and its partners: the Dow Jones gained 0.67% and the Nasdaq 0.33%.
According to data from the final deadline, the Dow Jones Industrial Average improved from 68.78 points to 10,405.83 points and the Nasdaq, with technological dominance from 7.13 points to 2190.06 points.
The Standard & Poor 500 index improved by 0.58% (6.40 points) to 1102.35 points.
We've got good news coming from different parts of the economy, Lindsey Piegz of FTN Financial commented.
But we can't take it too seriously, she added, because the market is concentrating on "half full glass."
For Peter Cardillo of Avalon Partners, investors focus primarily on U.S. trade figures, "which show that activity is improving in the fourth quarter."
The U.S. trade deficit fell by 7.6% in October compared to September, to $32.9 billion.
Analysts, by contrast, had expected an increase in the deficit.
There has been an increase in both imports and exports.
The big novelty is the growing volume of trade, especially exports of goods, which is a reflection of the growth of the economies of the main trading partners of the United States, said Christopher Cornell of research firm Economy.com.
However, in the United States last week, unemployment rose against all expectations, and the number of job seekers reached 474,000.
But over the past four weeks, the average number of new job seekers has fallen "to the lowest this year," said Scott Marcouiller of Wells Fargo Advisors.
Securities market has also declined.
Treasury's proceeds, overlooking the 10-year period, increased to 3,482% versus 3.423% on Tuesday evening, and the yield rose to 4,492% compared to 4,408% the day before.
Cuba : Hundreds of proponents of the regime booed "Ladies in White"
Several hundred proponents of the Cuban regime booed the wives of political prisoners, who met in Havana on the occasion of World Human Rights Day, and prevented another demonstration at which dozens of dissidents were arrested.
About forty "Dams in white", wives or close political prisoners were booed right on the street by proponents of Raula Castro's regime during their march towards the center of the capital with a request for the release of political prisoners, the journalist reported.
Away with the Yankees!, "Mercenaries!", "The streets belong to the revolutionaries!", "Long live Fidel and Raul!" chanted with a raised fist by proponents of the regime on dissidents.
Dissidents were eventually escorted by members in plainclothes who had arrived to "ensure security."
The day before, there was a similar rally in Havana against "Ladies in White", but there were no injuries or arrests.
But in Havana's Vedado district, about a dozen dissidents were apprehented, who were also preparing to make demonstrations on the occasion of World Human Rights Day on Thursday.
According to this journalist, the police loaded dissidents, who were bounced by proponents of the government's regime into civilian vehicles using force.
Arresting dissidents in similar conditions takes only a few hours from the rule.
Sakharov "Ladies in White" organized an unauthorised march for "the release of all, according to dissent, about 200 political prisoners," said one of their leaders, Laura Pollan, whose husband Héctor Maceda is serving a 20-year prison sentence.
Mr Maceda is one of a group of 75 Cuban dissidents arrested in March 2003, 53 of whom still remain behind bars.
Cuban authorities regard opponents as "mercenaries" or "agents" paid for by their arch-enemy, the United States.
Ready-made miracle, Cuban television brought news about Thursday's manifestation of proponents of the government regime against "opponents of the revolution."
Cuba, with the Communist Party at the forefront and one-party government policy, is regularly accused by European or U.S. non-governmental organizations of disrespecting human rights and freedoms, including freedom of speech.
Madman killed an 80-year-old man with a knife and wounded 5 people in Clichy-la-Garenne.
A madman armed with two knives killed an eighty-year-old man and wounded five other people in the town of Clichy-la-Garenne (Hauts-de-Seine) at peak times Thursday afternoon.
According to the preliminary results of the investigation, an attacker at the age of 28 allegedly assaulted with two knives and, for unknown reasons, an 80-year-old man at or in front of a pharmacy, at 85 Boulevard Jean-Jaures, such is a version of police and witnesses.
A severely injured old man, born in 1929, died of his injuries, according t to police syndicate of the Alliance in hospital.
In this pharmacy or in front of it, the attacker then assaulted a pregnant woman and her partner.
The husband, aged about thirty, began defending himself and was injured.
The first two seriously injured victims were admitted to the same hospital in which an eighty-year-old man died.
The life of the second injured person is in danger, according to police sources have apparently been affected by vivital organs.
The pregnant woman then collapsed and was taken to the hospital "in shock," a police source said.
An armed man, who was apparently under the influence of alcohol or drugs, according to a police source, then fled into the street, still to boulevard Jean Jaurès.
He injured two more people, apparently by accident: a thirty-year-old and forty-year-old man, the other seriously.
It's not entirely clear how all this happened, because it all happened in the evening.
He was armed with two butcher knives, which he carried with a cross behind his back, he said at the site head of the Syndicate of the Alliance in Hauts-de-Seine, Arnaud Pressé.
Several passers-by, including young people, according to testimony gathered by AFP, have tried to stop and disarm the madmen.
I've seen a thirty-year-old guy in a green jacket.
Three teenagers tried to catch him.
They failed because it was quite strong, Alberto, a waiter from a nearby bar, commented on the situation.
He was thrown by a crowd, including one municipal police officer who broke his wrist, he added.
Still on Boulevard Jean-Jaures, at number 61 level, two city police officers, including their commander, tried to detain an armed man while being injured with their fists, a police source said.
The incident took place in one of the main streets in Clichy-la-Garenne, where many shops are located.
Passers-by came to tell me there's an injured person outside.
I came out and saw a seventy-year-old man on the ground, put him in a safe place, and I noticed a large wound on my stomach, Frederic Allione, a thirty-five-year-old pharmacist, told AFP.
Five minutes later came the second about thirty-year-old person, who also had stabbing knife wounds.
A man who did not have identity documents was later arrested by the state police.
The prosecutor in Nanterre handed the matter over to Hauts-de-Seine (SDPJ 92).
The suspected attacker was taken to hospital Thursday night.
He's in custody, awaiting interrogation.
A police source has not ruled out placing him in a psychiatric hospital.
Another police source said six police officers were needed to handle him at Clichy's police station where he had been transported.
He was "completely beyond himself" as "all madmen of this type, whose strength increases tenfold in action."
U.S.: Polanski's lawyer asks to end prosecution again
Polish-French director Roman Polanski's lawyer again in Los Angeles (California, West of the United States) on Thursday filed for halting the prosecution against his client, who is currently in his Swiss residence because of a moral affair that occurred thirty years ago.
Chad Hummel, attorney for the film's "Pianist," criticized serious errors made in court at the time of the 1977 event.
I'm demanding a halt to the prosecution now and immediately, lawyer Hummel said, adding that of Laurence Rittenband's behaviour "had a chill in his back."
According to him, the judge (who had already died in between) allegedly negotiated with the public prosecutor, who told him that Roman Polanski deserved to go to prison – which is a serious offense against the rule of law.
"Roman Polanski, sought and desired" from the mouth of the public prosecutor, David Wells.
But Mr. Wells finally declared at the end of September that he "lied" to the author of the documentary.
In the event that the court refuses to halt the criminal proceedings, Lawyer Hummel will demand the conduct of a trial in which he will be able to present evidence of his claim.
The prosecutor reiterated what had already been said at the first state, namely that there would be no serious examination of the request to stop the prosecution until Roman Polanski appeared in court.
The 76-year-old director fled from the United States in 1978, before the court handed down a verdicule on "illegal sexual intercourse" with a 13-year-old minor.
Since then, he has never re-entered the United States.
A deputy prosecutor in Los Angeles, Phyllis Asayam, said Roman Polanski "should be there to take part in the trial."
In this case, we want to say not only Romana Polaski, but also to other defendants that it is possible to escape? asks Ms. Asayam.
He is of the opinion that the director "asks a favor from the trial and at the same time despises his authority."
One of the three judges, who now have 90 days to take a decision, also stressed "that there are other alternatives to escape."
Hummel's lawyer was not the only one requesting a halt to Roman Polanski's prosecution.
The victim's attorney, Samantha Geimer, has also once again demanded an end to the prosecution.
She recalled that her client could no longer bear the perpetual harass every time the Polanski case emerged, adding: "No one in this room can say that the trial was fair."
Thirty-two years, that's enough."
Roman Polanski was arrested on September 26 in Switzerland at the behest of the United States
After more than two months behind bars, he has been placed under house arrest at his cottage in Gstaad, where he is awaitinging possible expulsion.
Wall Street ends without direction, hampered by a rising dollar
The New York Stock Exchange ended undirected on Friday, with the market swirling among indicators that are better than expected in the U.S., and a rising dollar price: the Dow Jones gained +0.63%, but the Nasdaq lost 0.03%.
According to data from the final deadline, the Dow Jones Industrial Average advanced from 65.67 points to 10.471.50 points and the Nasdaq, with technological dominance left 0.55 points and finished at 2.190.31 points.
The Standard & Poor's 500 index increased from 0.37% (4.06 points) to 1.106.41 points.
Frankly, the market is very calm, said Mace Blicksilver of Marblehead Asset Management.
It remains in a narrow range, the volume of trade has declined greatly and I think it will remain that way until the end of the year.
This trend has also been confirmed by the publication of economic indicators that are better than expected and which have "helped to improve the mood in the market when it comes to the stability of the economic recovery," summarised analyst Charles Schwab.
In the U.S., retail sales rose 1.3% in November for the second consecutive month and much more than expected.
According to a preliminary estimate from the University of Michigan index, U.S. consumer confidence also returned, rising strongly to 73.4 in December against 67.4 in the previous month.
The indices, however, were hampered by a new rise in the dollar's price against the euro, which is the highest in two months, which "pushes downward sectors sensitive to the economic situation," said Owen Fitzpatrick of Deutsche Bank.
Technological stocks are thus losing ground.
Securities market has declined.
The state's treasury yield, overlooking a 10-year outlook, rose to 3.540% versus 3.482% on Wednesday evening and, with the outlook for 30, increased to 4.497% versus 4.492% a day earlier.
Miss World elections again in South Africa.
After a month of traveling across South Africa, 112 candidates for the Miss World title on Saturday are preparing to cross the finish line to achieving their dream: being voted the world's most beautiful woman in front of a billion TV viewers.
This great celebration of beauty has been held for the second year around Johannesburg and for the time being five candidates have won the semi-finals.
Miss Japan Eruza Sasaki, Perla Beltran Acosta from Mexico, Mariatu Kargbo of Sierra Leone, Kaiane Aldorino of Gilbratar and Yanbing Ma from Canada were voted Miss Sport or Miss top model during their African stay.
It was a real whirlwind of events.
It was amazing to visit beautiful towns such as Port Elizabeth,Durban or Cape Town.
I've seen a lot of things and I've had so many experiences!, Miss Sierra Leone confided.
The competition, which takes place in South Africa for the ninth time, will be broadcast live from Midrand, northern Johannesburg, from 17 o'clock (15 o'clock world time).
I couldn't dream of a more beautiful country in which to put a crown.
It's been a fascinating experience.
I'm happy I've been able to experience it twice, Confidenced by Miss Ksenia Sukhin, in the hot southern summer.
For a young Russia, her second stay in South Africa was in the spirit of numerous encounters with the other candidates and the new discovery of the country and its "landscape, which takes the breath away."
After a month of performances, rehearsals, meetings with the press and safari, Miss is now concentrating on the competition itself.
Indian Pooja Chopra hopes to take the steps of Priyanka Chopra, elected in 2000, who is also taking part in Aishwarye Rai's election, the 1994 winner, both of whom have become Bollywood stars.
Being crowned as the most beautiful woman in the world is every woman's dream, but it's not just about appearance.
We're ambassadors of our countries and I want to come home with a crown, says Miss India with a broad smile.
According to the organizers, the 59th year will be carried in the spirit of music and dance.
The candidates will be presented in traditional costumes and, of course, in swimsuits.
The performance will be presented by Chinese television presenter Angela Chow, former South African beauty queen Michelle McLean and Miss World Committee Chairman Steve Douglas.
South Africa has showned that it can welcome the world with warmth that is hard to cope with, said Julia Morley, president of the Miss World competition.
The candidates, like last year, participated in safari, visited the famous town of Soweto, this time on a bicycle, and tasted the local cuisine.
An extraordinary event was a meeting with South African President Jacob Zuma, who shook their hands.
Unusual for the president, Miss Sierra Leone remarked.
Undoubtedly the most most beautiful memory will remain the draw in Cape Town (in the southest of the country) for the World Cup, which will take place in June 2010, and which has helped the country to support the event a little more.
I was able to meet people I didn't even dream about.
I'll definitely go back to the 2010 World Cup, says Alice Taticchi with the enthusiasm of Miss Italy.
According to David's son, reports on Hallyday's condition are "very positive."
Reports of Johnny Hallyday's condition are "very positive," as his son David assured us on his Friday arrival at Los Angeles Airport, where he flew to visit his father, who has been hospitalized for several days.
The reports are very positive.
He is very strong and everything is going very well, David Hallyday said when he got off the plane, arriving from Paris shortly after 13 o'clock local time (21 o'clock world time)
The singer's son, who appeared calmly, said he would "come as quickly as possible" to Los Angeles' Cedars-Sinaï Hospital, to which Johnny Hallyday was admitted on Monday because of a post-operative infection resulting from a prolapsed intervertebrate disc surgery, which he underwent in Paris on November 26.
According to a press statement, the singer's night in Los Angele for convenience was "put into an artificial sleep" designed to prevent pain and make it easier to administer treatment.
Johnny Hallyday has already been put into artificial sleep by Cedars-Sinaï Hospital in Los Angeles because of an infection after a prolapsed intervertebral disc surgery on November 26 in Paris.
Due to the serious damage caused by this intervention, the singer had to be operated again on the night from Wednesday to Thursday.
According to AFP's information, his wife Laeticia attended a school performance of a French lyceum in Los Angeles visited by their daughter Jade on Friday morning.
The priority of the Department of Orthopedic Surgery and Traumatology of La Pitié-Salpêtrière, Yves Catoné, announced that he was leaving for the singer in Los Angeles on Monday.
Producer harshly accuses Dr Stéphan Delajoux, a neurosurgeon who operated the singer on November 26 at Clinique internationale du parc Monceau in Paris.
They told us it was a massacre.
Surgeons from Los Angeles said they were outraged, M. Camus said.
I've heard about the possibility of a future trial, he said.
Jean-Claude Camus believes Johnny Hallyday "has gone too quickly to Los Angeles with the permission of his Parisan surgeon."
Personally, I spoke with the doctor by phone and he assured me that this trip is not a problem, which surprised me greatly, because I myself have already twice suffered from the prolapse of the intervertebral disc, he added.
Moments earlier on the RTL radio station, he stated that the singer had apparently flowned off after the Paris operation without a drain, without anything."
Dr Stéphane Delajoux is well-known in artistic circles.
He operated actress Maria Trintignant in Vilnius a few days before her death at a clinic in Neuilly-sur-Seine, where she was active at then.
In 2007 he also operated on actress Charlotte Gainsbourg for the removal of a hematoma resulting from bleeding in the brain.
However, he is also a doctor with a dark reputation, several times convicted in cases of neglect of medical care, tax evasion and fraud.
Olivier Metzner, an attorney at the medical chamber, described him as a "man without scruples," who is "regularly" summoned in front of the medical chamber, and which can't be "far from recommended."
After several days of silence, Dr Stéphane Delajoux commented on the matter via his attorney, David Koubbi, on Friday.
He announced that the procedure had "an absolutely good course" and post-operative examinations were "normal."
The operation was not hemorrhagical in nature, therefore the introduction of a drain was not necessary," he added.
The singer's health problems have been growing since this summer, and in any case his next tour appears to remain with a question mark.
It is clear that the date of the resumption of the planned tour on January 8 appears too close, Camus said.
The UDF Association raises the tone and calls on the Nouveau Centre party not to continue enjoying their acronym.
The UDF association, controlled by MoDemFrançois Bayrou's party, on Friday responded to an attempt by Nouveau Centre's party's president, Hervé Morin, to appropriate the former Giscardo's party's acronym, saying he was not allowed to use the shortcut in any medium under the threat of prosecution.
Hervé Morin has publicly announced his intention to adjoirn the abbreviation UDF, saying François Bayrou does not deserve to carry his inheritance after handing the left.
The defense minister, whose party is a UMP partner, planned to confirm this strategy on Saturday before the Nouveau Centre (NC).
His move received support from Hervý de Charetta (former za-UMP now on behalf of NC), who attributes UDF brand ownership because he was the first to register it at the National Industrial Property Institute (l'Institut national de la propriété industrielle) in 2004.
But on Friday night, a committee of the UDF Association run by François Bayrou's friends, who have no intention of being expropriated, came out of his silence and filed a formal request to both the minister and parliamentarians to stop using the abbreviation and handing back ownership of the trademark, under the threat of prosecution.
In recent days, you’ve raised disturbing statements about proposing to take over UDF’s political movement + abbreviated, the association writes in a letter signed by 19 of the committee’s 24 members, such as François Bayrou and Marielle de Sarnez by MoDem or Senator Jean Arthuis of the Alliance centriste.
At the same time, you considered it appropriate to place on your promotional materials and on the welcome website of your movement the name+Nouveau center+, with the slogan +UDF of today+, are also stated by the signatories of the letter.
As you know, you have no right to use this acronym, explains the UDF Committee, which asks the NC movement "immediately to remove all mentions of the UDF name from all promotional materials, including (its) websites".
At the same time, Hervé de Charette is asking "to hand over ownership of the trademark to legitimate owners."
If you fail to do so, we will appeal to the courts to respect the law and the legitimate rights of UDF members, the signatories point out.
In their letter, they state that UDF "is a full-fledged political movement since 1978 "and recall that Mr. Morin and his friends "chose "to leave in 2007 "to create a competitive movement, as others did in 2002, to establish a UMP.
According to them, the Nouveau center has no right to use the acronym UDF (Union pour la démocratie française).
At the same time, they recall that the last UDF Congress voted in 2007 to join MoDem for three years and for the creation of a committee commissioned to defend the "moral and material interests" of an association founded by Valéry Giscard d'Estaing.
As for Hervé de Charette, the committee continues, "when it has registered a trademark", he has not been "a member of UDF for three years", but an UMP.
In a scandalous and fraudulent manner, he used the non-existent acronym registration to do so on his own account.
This intactness begins to be transformed into a criminal offence, they declare, and then, with irony, comment on NC's strategy: "It seems to us that, both in politics and in everyday life, it is more valuable to create a name for itself than to emerge from anonymity by fraudulently accepting a name to which we have no right."
Liliane Bettencourt's case: François-Marie Banier will be tried to abuse a state of weakness.
A court in Nanterre on Friday decided to sue artist Françoise-Marie Banier and pass judgment on whether he took advantage of the eighty-year-old billionaire's state of weakness to deprive her of part of her fortune, accused by her daughter Liliane Bettencourt.
The photographer (62), known for his photographs of the famous, will be tried on April 15 and 16, 2010 for the abuse of a state of weakness, an offence for which a sentence of a maximum duration of 3 years and a fine of 375 000 euros is imposed.
This decision confirms that there will indeed be a trial in the case, which can be described as the family saga of one of the wealthiest families in France, in which a mother and daughter attack each other each other and fight to manage the cosmetic empire L'Oréal.
At the same time, however, the 15th Criminal Court ordered a medical examination of 87-year-old Liliana Bettencourt, which should be carried out by a college of three doctors.
The heiress and principal shareholder of L'Oréal has always refused to undergo such an examination, certainly not under the conditions offered by the prosecutor's office in Nanterre during the investigation.
Doctors who will be elected on Friday are expected to prepare a report by March 10, 2010, while giving a statement as to whether Liliana Bettencourt may have found herself in a state of weakness and in what period.
I think he'll undergo it.
I'll discuss the matter with her, Ms Bettencourt's attorney, Georges Kiejman, said after the hearing.
As far as Françoise Bettencourt-Meyers, Olivier Metzner's lawyer, he welcomed the court's decision.
It’s excellent news(...). Expertise will show that with this old lady in need of protection (M. Banier), he confirmed.
It has been two years since Liliana Bettencourt's only daughter initiated criminal proceedings against François-Marie Banier, a photographer and all-artist whom she blames for many years using her mother's state of weakness in order to earn a gift of a billion euros in the form of cheques, life insurance contracts or the images of masters.
Liliane Bettencourt, on the other hand, has always refused to be manipulated by a photographer she considers to be a long-time friend.
While the court had to assess the legitimacy of the lawsuit filed by Ms Bettencourt-Meyers, Friday's lawyer's testimony sometimes turned into a family hour of truth.
Liliana Bettencourt's lawyer submitted three letters that his client (in 1999, 2003 and 2005) had sent to his notary, in which she expressed her distrust towards Françoisa Bettencourt-Meyers' husband.
Liliane Bettencourt, according to an attorney, adds, “you could explain to my daughter that I’ve been texting Banier once or twice a day for fifteen years.
I've never been able to talk to Françoise since she got married."
The public prosecutor's office in Nanterre once again found that the complainant's actions were not justified, all the more so because the guardianship judge in those days dismissed her request to initiate proceedings to issue legal protection for her mother.
Liliane Bettencourt "is completely able to participate in the trial, she is not under protection even in custody.
No one else will act for it,” said Marie-Christine Daubigney.
The court ultimately ruled that it would comment on Ms Bettencourt-Meyers's legitimacy at the time the trial began.
The management revolution or the end of economic intelligence?
If nothing changes in the management of the great French companies, our struggle for the development of economic intelligence has been unnecessary...
For more than a decade, the behavior of the management of large companies towards employees reminds the approach of companies towards consumers before the marketing revolution: it is a matter for co-workers to identify with the "values" determined from the mountain, as was the case before, when it was necessary to impose on the consumer the products that arose from the ideas of the heads of factory technicians.
By becoming the center of business companies, the marketing revolution has leveled the strategy of conquesting the market in the right direction...although authoritarian efforts to impose products, different from customer preferences, regularly return!
The passive approach of employees can lead to deadly demoralization.
Suicides at France Telecom, PSA or Renault are just a visible part of a much more widespread general phenomenon that hits mostly management cadres.
This demobilization adversely influences the development of all the intellectual functions of society, including R&D, and, more importantly, economic intelligence.
Indeed, the EI "requires an active approach and the ability to anticipate" from all stakeholders (...) and requires liabilities from employees that are completely contrary to the revenues of authoritarian management.
That is why we call for a management revolution, which aims to put workers at the center of the interest of the company as well as the client.
Relative usefulness of internal opinion polls.
Internal opinion polls - as a questionnaire submitted to Orange employees - will contribute to a revolution in management practices?
Unfortunately, I'm afraid not.
Mainly because I have already convinced myself that in the face of a new situation, the structure, after various turns, will return preferentially to what it has always done!
Furthermore, authoritarianism, as one of the pathologies of power, increases in proportion to the seriousness of one's own failures.
And finally, because most managers have a simplified view of human behavior.
Because they are university graduates for engineers, they have a mechanical interpretation of psychology.
Because of their intellectual convenience, they adopt an old behavioral theory, currently revived by "neurosciences" from the U.S. and widely disseminated without much knowledge by scientific institutes and the media.
With the fact that it is possible to move "levers" (sic) involvement of managers, a recent survey by Ipsos showed on the website Figaro.fr, which only confirms executives in their position of mechanical control, unsuitable for today's world.
Identify and measure mobilization factors.
On the contrary, the management revolution necessary for the proper functioning of modern enterprises presupposes the acceptance of the ultimate vision of human behavior.
It consists in integrating basic observation into all the intentions and practices of management : For employees, commitments to the enterprise will be possible only if they appear as a clear means of achieving their dreams, ambitions and projects.
At the moment, only studies of Motivationally functional analysis deal with this concept of employee motivation.
These studies have shown that it evaluates its businesses on the basis of a predetermined doxy that will enable them, through an image that tells them about the business, to evaluate what it offers them to realize their life goals.
Thanks to the identification and measurement of these mobilization factors, managers can control, anticipate and anticipate on their "social performances" and put their human resources management "on the right path".
This creates a breeding ground for the development of solid and effective economic intelligence.
Speaking from Oslo: 'fair war' Obama has earned praise from the right.
On Friday, the U.S
Conservatives, who initially mocked the Obama award on October 9, this time applauded the presidential speech, including Sarah Palin, a former vice presidential nominee in the 2008 election.
I liked what he had said, Palin, a muse of the right-wing Republican wing, told USA Today, adding that she had commented on the issue of "sometimes necessary" wars in their memoirs that are just coming out.
War is, of course, the last thing Americans would like to have to do, but it's necessary.
All terrorists need to be detained there, he believes.
President Obama, who inherited two wars in Afghanistan and Iraq after his predecessor, George W. Bush, justified the deployment of forces on Thursday in Oslo and used the speech to clarify his decision to send an additional 30,000 troops to Afghanistan nine days earlier.
Obama's other adversary, Newt Gingrich, evaluated that the president did a "very good job" in Oslo.
I think the speech was really very good, congratulated the former Speaker of the House of Representatives, who especially appreciated the president's mention of "the existence of evil in the world".
In a way, I considered this speech completely historical, Gingrich declared during an interview on "The Takeaway" radio.
The former MP, whom some consider a possible Republican nominee in the 2012 presidential election, was thrilled to see a "left-wing president" reminding members of the Nobel Prize committee that they themselves wouldn't be free and couldn't pass on the prize for peace if the use of forces didn't exist."
According to Michael Gerson, former speech author George W. Bush, now an editor of the Washington Post, Obama delivered a "very U.S. speech."
Not only did he introduce himself as a "world citizen" as always, but "he "spoke as U.S. president" and reminded Europeans "that they are living in a safe bubble that they haven't created themselves, and that they don't do too much to preserve it," Gerson appraised.
Among the few discontents of the right is the Bush era hawk, former U.N. Ambassador John Bolton, who described the speech as "monotonous, pompous and uninspired," according to National Review Online.
To the left from the president, for example, Democratic representative Dennis Kucinich is horrified at looking at Barack Obama for justifying the use of forces.
“If we wrap the war in justice, we can easily justify the massacre of innocent people,” he said.
However, the medium-left-left press takes a commendatory position on the president.
Although the Los Angeles Times continues to consider it premature to award peace to Obama, he assessed his output as "over-performance" even for a speaker knowned by his talent.
The speech from Oslo should serve as a guide to international decisions in the resolution of conflicts, in the fight against poverty and oppression," the California-based daily reports.
Obama "unveoked the speech as necessary," the New York Times reviewed, calling the speech "simple and instructive."
Road carriers: three marathons to an agreement that will save the supply of supplies for Christmas.
After three negotiating marathons, road carrier unions and TLF, the onling employers' organisation that remained until the end, reached an agreement on Friday night that ended a call for a strike and a threat that hung over the supply of supplies for Christmas.
The Secretary of State for Transport, Dominique Bussereau, expressed "joy" during Friday's signing of the agreement after several negotiations that began on Wednesday morning.
After the second night of discussions, the negotiators broke up early on Friday morning with the hope that the wording of the agreement would be signed as quickly as possible at the end of the morning.
This is despite the fact that three organizations of employers (FNTR, Unostra, OTRE) withdrew from the negotiations.
But it took more than six hours of the meeting, which began at 11 a.m. for everyone to come to an agreement and at 18 o'clock signing an agreement.
This conflict "would hurt businesses greatly" and "there would be a danger that the holidays at the end of the year will be disrupted," Bussereau said.
The text contains an increase in wages by 4% for the lowest salaries (for local and regional road transport) and 2.9% for the highest salaries (international transport).
The hourly wage for the lowest salaries increases from 8.72 euros to 9.06.
It's not much, but there's a lot of effort behind it, Patrice Clos (FO) explained.
Tonight we're going to order our members not to cause blockades, no blockades of supply centers.
But there may still be a few opponents who aren't satisfied," he added.
Philippe Grillot, president of TLF, welcomed the "right deal," although it was hard to admit to raising high salaries given the international competition ".
According to him, there will be an increase of 170 euros per month for the lowest wages and 100 to 120 euros for the highest.
It's an agreement of common sense and respect for employees, CMaxime Dumont (CFDT) said, while Thierry Douine (CFTC) welcomed a "historic deal."
According to Richard Jaubert (CGT), this is an "acceptable compromise."
The agreement also foresees a 3% revalorisation of travel expenses and a report on the situation of road transport in the first half of 2010, which will also allow a focus on the issue of the 13th salary and the modernisation of the collective agreement.
Bussereau recalled "a reduction of 100 million euros in taxes on the wages of businesses", which was the subject of the government's amendment to the 2009 bill, on which the National Assembly voted on the night from Thursday to Friday.
The government will ask the Senate for an "sample" vote.
The signature allows this agreement to apply first to businesses that are members of that organization, later to the whole field, in the event of an extension by the Ministry of Labor.
It will take place "in the shortestest possible time," Bussereau assured.
The whole process can hinder only the disagreement of employers' organizations.
FNTR speaks of "bad trade" because 100 million euros have already been offered by the sector as part of the carbon tax.
The Secretary of State for Transport forced us to conduct an emergency, not as a result of mutual analysis, but in order not to angry turkey eaters at the time of the approaching Christmas holidays, Unostra complained.
For Otre, there is a risk that the agreement will "bury a large number of small and medium-sized enterprises in the industry" in the next 12 months.
Suburban trains RER A: interruption of transport at the weekend, unions dissatisfied with proposals of the transport company RATP.
The interruption of traffic on suburban RER A train lines, which is the largest public transport line in France, will continue over the weekend, as Friday's negotiations between unions and RATP management on the second day of the strike did not lead to any concrete results on the issue of asking for a premium.
On Saturdays and Sundays, i.e. the days of an important shopping weekend before Christmas, trains will run only between 10 a.m
The connection with SNCF will be interrupted in Nanterre Préfecture and passengers will be forced to transfer to another train.
Galeries Lafayette department stores, which are expecting more than 200 000 customers every day, welcomed the fact that "RATP was trying to connect with large department stores".
The RER A line runs from the west to the east of the Paris agglomeration.
The section managed by RATP runs from Saint-Germain-en-Laye and Nanterre to Boissy-Saint-Léger and Marne-La-Vallée.
On Friday, one train out of two was in operation at rush hour, and virtually no rig was running off-peak, as planned.
As on Thursday, the inhabitants of Ile de France arranged differently and there was no confusion on the platforms.
So early in the morning there were far fewer people at Saint-Germain-en-Laye (Yvelines), among other things because pupils and students were missing.
Jack Nbakina, an engineer, for 29 years from Boissy-Saint-Léger (Val-de-Marne) explained that he had risened an "hour and a half early to make sure he'd arrive on time."
Saint-Lazare station was not crowded, the SNCF state railway announced, because a larger number of orders and guardians were predicted.
Suburban RER A trains operated by SNCF in the direction from or to Cergy-Le-Haut arriving or departing from Saint-Lazare Station with a connection to the metro.
Further general rallies of the strikers will be held on Saturday and Sunday, at which the results of Friday afternoon's meeting with the leadership will be evaluated as "weak."
The management of the RER proposes negotiations on a special protocol for RER A suburban trains in all aspects, adjustment of working hours, course of career, etc.
However, this is not the case for drivers who demand a well-deserved premium, according to Thierry Garon (UnionsJih).
RATP has even postponed proposals on the premium for the time being, offsetting the CGT, saying the management "was unable to solve the problem, while for travel carriers and SNCF it was possible."
At a new meeting scheduled for Monday, "the conditions for the exercise of the profession will be further reviewed," the RATP transport companies clarified.
Which was the reason why trade unions think the business "makes fun of weekend travelers."
In the morning, about 150 drivers occupied the corporate committee at its Paris headquarters.
The leadership, which until now rejected any discussion during the strike, has therefore decided for a meeting in the interest of "calming down".
There is a danger that the conflict will spread to the RER B line on Tuesday, after Unsa and the Independents announce a strike emergency.
The unions are asking for a premium worth between 120 (Unsa) and 150 euros, including 30 euros of variables (CGT, FO, CFDT, Sud (South), Indépendants (Independents)) and make it more difficult to have deteriorating working conditions on this line, which carries a million passengers on a daily basis.
RER suburban train drivers earn 2200 to 2700 euros of gross wages and 600 to 650 euros of premiums for irregular working hours and for work on Sundays and holidays.
The Turkish judiciary has dissolved for the Kurdish side, and this decision entails severe consequences.
Turkey's Constitutional Court on Friday ruled to overthrow the country's main pro-Kurdish party. The decision immediately triggered protest demonstrations in the Kurdish community in the country's south-east and Istanbul.
This decision can greatly complicate the task of a government that seeks to lend a friendly hand to the Kurdish community and thus try to end 25 years of conflict.
The Party for the Democratic Society (DTP) has been dissolved because it has become "the nest of activities threatening the independence of the state and its indivisible unity," Supreme Court President Hasim Kilic told reporters after four days of negotiations.
The decision was taken unanimously by 11 judges, when a majority-seven votes were needed to dissolve the party, Kiliç said, adding that 37 party cadres, including its president Ahmet Türk, and Aysela Tuglukova, were banned from taking part in political life for a period of five years.
At the same time, the judge announced the abolition of parliamentary immunity enjoyed by Mr Türk and Ms Tugluk and the confiscation of the party's assets by the Ministry of Finance.
The DTP has 21 seats in Parliament (out of 500).
The party's leadership warned before the verdict that MPs preferred to leave Parliament rather than act as MPs without political affiliation.
The decision is a continuation of the trial launched in 2007 by prosecutor Abdurrahman Yalcinkay, who accuses DTP of obedience to "commands" by the Workers' Party of Kurdistan (PKK), which Ankara and many countries consider a terrorist organization.
Many observers are convinced that the DTP is a legal political showcase for the PKK rebels.
The party assures that it has no "basical connection" with the PKK, but refuses to label it a terrorist organization and calls on the government to negotiate with it.
The verdict of the court comes as the government gives a friendly hand to the Kurdish community and offers a number of measures to strengthen their rights and attempts to curb the support of Kurds against the PKK and end the conflict.
The DTP leadership has confirmed that dissolving the party could lead to a resumption of tensions in the south-east of Anatolia, where many demonstrations have degenerated in recent weeks against the conditions under which PKK leader Abdullah Öcalan is detained into clashes with police.
Since announcing the decision of justice, thousands of protesters have gathered outside the DTP headquarters in Diyarbakir, a large city in the south-east inhabited mostly by Kurds.
Police used tear grenades and water cannons, protesters in turn threw Molotov cocktails or shot stones from slingshots.
About a hundred people also demonstrated in Istanbul.
After the verdict, Mr Türk stated that the decision of justice would only "deepen despair."
Turkey cannot resolve this issue by banning the party, he said in the press.
The government, made up of a party from the Islamist movement, also condemned this decision.
We're out of principle against party interference.
It doesn't solve anything, energy minister Taner Yildiz said.
This decision is an uttered blast of democratic proposals launched by the government, political scientist Ahmet Insel commented.
Of course, we can wait for the PKK's reaction, he added.
On Thursday, the PKK claimed responsibility for one of Turkey's deadliest attacks, killing seven soldiers three days earlier.
Snow can come
Quite crunching winter, that's what Wolfgang Brauburger wants.
Not because he wants to bully citizens, says the head of FES's winter technical services department.
But so that his employees can try and learn to control all vehicle technology.
This could happen for the first time this weekend, according to meteorologists.
More than 300 employees and 120 vehicles are prepared to take care of snow and ice.
At the same time, the FES is proceeding according to a precise plan.
As it snows all-over in Frankfurt and temperatures drop below zero, they will roll out to 20 so-called sections of a large-scaler, Brauburger explains.
Among these sections are the main and drive-throws like Neue Weg and Hanauer Landstraße.
But also arrivals for firefighters, suburban public transport lines, pedestrian zones and sections with descents like Atzelbergstraße.
The FES service begins at three o'clock in the morning.
If necessary, it is necessary to sprinkle with moist salt, which holds better, about 1040 kilometers within a maximum of three hours.
That's why the FES starts at three o'clock at night "to keep the roads free when transport to work begins."
It's difficult when it starts to snow only at 7.30 a.m.
Then we have a problem, says the head of the winter technical services department, "because no one is willing to make a place for sprinklers."
Only when it doesn’t snow so much are the driveways and interconnections, which also include Rat-Beil-Straße.
The last priority is side and side roads.
Brauburger knows they'll be complaining.
At five o'clock in the morning, FES pawns set off on their way, preferentially cleaning up dangerous places and crossings.
FES usually can't take care of all bike paths.
There's a special service in Frankfurt that monitors mainly bridges from 22 to 4.30, Brauburger says.
As an ice glacier forms quickly on them, sprinklers roll out as soon as the temperature drops below two degrees.
If necessary, they will also be called by the police.
Dinosaur Cradle
Tawa hallae looked like plenty of other carnivorous dinosaurs: An animal two metres long and 1.50 meters high rubbed on two thick hind limbs, had an elongated skull with sharp, curved teeth, short forearms with sickle claws and a long tail.
If this species had lived a few million years later, there would have been nothing extraordinary.
Sterling Nesbitt of the University of Texas in Austin and colleagues wrote in Thursday's edition of the journal Science that Tawa hallae was one of the first dinosaurs with these body characteristics.
The animal lived about 215 million years ago in present-day New Mexico, which was then located in the equatorial dry region of the Pangea supercontinent.
Tawa is opening a new window to the earlier evolution of dinosaurs, Nesbitt says.
It shows us a lot about knitting relationships, extensions and signs of early dinosaurs.
Nesbitt and other paleontologists began excavations in Ghost Ranch in northern New Mexico in 2006.
Sometimes we found a dozen dinosaur bones in one day, it's unseen for triassic settlements, reports team member Randall Irmis of the Museum of Natural History in Utah.
Some of the bones were quickly identified by scientists as a new species.
During excavations, they collected the remains from a total of five individuals.
The finding confirms theories about the early history of dinosaurs.
"Tawa" comes from the sun's god Hopi.
It's an unusual finding for Sterling Nesbitt: "Fossils of dinosaurs from the triassium are very rare, mostly only individual debris are found."
Other dinosaur experts don't share this enthusiasm without reservation.
This finding is not particularly sensational, but it confirms some theories about the early history of dinosaurs, says Martin Sander of the University of Bonn.
According to the current scientific point of view, dinosaurs appeared for the first time about 230 million years ago.
However, many of the oldest fossils are not complete, so the assignment of pradinosaurs is usually questionable.
Experts discuss, for example, when lizards were divided into the three most important lines of development: carnivorous theropodes, which later included Tyranosaurus rex, Velociraptor and also birds, "long-frequency" four-legged sauropods and their relatives, and herbivores Ornithischia (thypocano), which included, for example
Tawa hallae is, after analyzing Nesbitto and his colleagues, the original terapod.
It exhibits an interesting combination of primitive and advanced qualities, Nesbitt says.
On Tawa hallae, for example, researchers found evidence of aerial bags, which the descendants of teropod birds also have.
Thanks to airy bags, the breathing of birds is more effective than mammals.
At Tawa hallae, the spinal bones were clearly partially filled with air.
Tawa is the oldest and most primitive teropod with air bags, Randall Irmis says.
Since the related line of huge sauropods later also had air bags and light bones, this useful innovation was probably created by the common ancestor of both groups.
At least three migration waves to North America
Because the most primitive dinosaurs so far lived in South America, the researchers concluded that the lizard appeared there for the first time.
According to this, the three lines of development were divided soon and spread from South America to all continents, which at that time, as the supercontinent of Pangea formed a single earth mass.
It would also speak for the fact that the team, along with Tawa hallae, found the remains of two other, not particularly closely related, theropods.
Those three can't come from one common, migrated ancestor, scientists argue.
We believe there have been at least three migration waves to North America, says Alan Turner of the American Museum of Natural History in New York, co-author of a scientific study.
Of course, there are also contradictions.
One even older species, Silesaurus, which belongs to a group of the heaviest dinosaurs, lived in present-day Poland, says Bonna paleontologist Martin Sander.
His presumption could put together a distorted image, as very many primitive dinosaur fossils have been preserved in South America, rarely found on other continents.
As the dinosaurs proved to have evolved from a relatively rare secondary line to the ruling vertebrates on earth during the Earth's Middle Ages, it still remains a puzzle.
During the triasis, the dinosaurs shared their living space with many other reptiles, for example the ancestors of today's crocodiles and flying lizards and countless other lizards.
Roughly towards the end of the triasis, dinosaurs have spread around the world and many new species have evolved, while relatives have died out and the triassic and jury interface.
The history of success didn't last forever
There are two hypotheses to explain this change, says Martin Sander.
There could have been a mass extinction that the dinosaurs accidentally survived, but reptiles didn't.
Or the dinosaurs adapted better, so they could push out the competition.
The history of success didn't last forever anyway.
150 million years later, the impact of a meteorite helped another group of animals, which also evolved in the triasus and lived in the shade ever since: mammals.
Solidarity from Vienna to Sao Paulo
The list is long and the signatories are international.
On the list are the professors of the University of Toronto, the University of Cambridge, the Viennese university or Berkeley in California.
Associates from Naples, Quebec, Edinburgh, New York, Sao Paulo, Berlin or Bremen.
The police evacuationation of Frankfurt Goethe University's occupied casino ensured a worldwide commotion.
278 professors and teachers of Goethe University, but also colleges across Germany, Europe and North and South America, are in solidarity with protesting students on the Westend campus.
In a statement written in German and English, they "don't "approve" the presidium-instigated police crackdown.
Because the walls and works of art were scribbled, University President Werner Müller-Estler had the building occupied last week occupied by striking students and lecturers.
Five students were injured.
Damaging the matter, the signatories say, do not justify "the violent dissolution of self-organized teaching of students and teachers by police commandos."
The evacuation represents an "unacceptable interference" in the freedoms of research and science.
Exclusive focus on vandalism serves to "unauthorise and criminalize protest" and distracts from content.
The presidium avoided the necessary discussion.
Lecturers are calling on a halt to criminal prosecutions.
It is claimed that most of the strikers did not support substantive damage.
Justified protest
Verdi and the German Trade Union are also in solidarity with students.
Protest is a legitimate expression of growing concern for one's own future, says Verdi's secretary, Alexander Klein.
Hessian Science Minister Eva Kühne-Hörmann (CDU) is yet to defend the actions of the university president and the police.
We're grateful to the university president.
Destruction and occupancy are completely unacceptable means, she said on Thursday at Aktuellen Stunde (Current Hour) at the Wiesbaden Diet.
We have an understanding of the protest, but we have no understanding of vandalism, Peter Beuth, a member of the country's assembly for the CDU, said.
Opposition factions of the SPD, the Greens and Linke saw it otherwise.
The CDU is taking painted walls beyond the pretext to criminalize the protest, Janine Wissler (Linke) said.
Instead of dealing with the arguments and demands of the learners, there has been evacuation.
Linke has filed a motion calling on the Home Secretary to report on police deployment.
New Silicon-Valley
For many years it paid for a secret city, inaccessible to visitors from the west.
Zelenograd, one of the ten counties of Moscow's capital, in the Soviet Union it was a strictly guarded location where secret weapons research was carried out.
Today, this county with 216,000 inhabitants, 37 kilometers from the Moscow center, is considered "Russian Silicon Valley".
There are important micro- and nanoelectronic research and production centers.
Since September 2008, the Greenograding representatives have established contacts with the Riedberg Scientific Centre in Frankfurt - with the University institutions and companies of the Frankfurt Innovation Centre (FIZ).
There were a number of mutual visits and on Wednesday they signed a "Protocol of Close Collaboration" in Frankfurt and Ridberg.
The Russian delegation was led by Moscow multi-mayor Yuri Roslyak and Zelenograd prefect Anatoly Smirnov, the leading planning officer Edwin Schwarz signed for Frankfurt.
In 2010, representatives of FIZ and small and medium-sized enterprises engaged in scientific research will go to a seminar in Zelenograd.
The Scientific Exchange
This is an ongoing scientific exchange.
But it is also about economic relations.
FIZ companies will be allowed to establish themselves in the growing Russian market.
On the contrary, Russian scientific companies want to find contracts in Germany.
Zelenograd is the seat of major Russian research institutes and societies.
These include the Moscow Institute of Electron Technism, the large Sitronics and Rossijskaya Electronics.
The district was founded in 1958 as a so-called "socialist planned city" for weapons research.
Zelenograd (literal translation "Green City") got its name according to the wooded hilly landscape, into which research facilities are planted.
The Stuttgart Brake
The Stuttgart 21 dimension cannot be sufficiently prepared.
For supporters of the project, it is quite simply the "new heart of Europe".
Well, the S21 is more than the start-up of the station, more than the monstrous building that plunges the capital of Baden-Württemberg into desperate building chaos for one decade.
Stuttgart 21 originated from the spirit of the 1980s.
Head stations with tracks that ended up in the city center were at that time considered transport-technical fossils of the 19th century, which act as brakes of modern rail transport, because it took an enormous amount of time to switch locomotives from the front.
Go, stop for a while, move on - that's how it should work.
And that's whay the headstatones are disappearing: in Frankfurt and Munich.
And in Stuttgart: the transit station, which lies about halfway between the trans-European railway station between Paris and Budapest/Bratislava - that's why the slogan about the "heart of Europe".
It really starts with the S21, although the visions of the 1980s are no longer compatible with reality.
Very ambitious designers themselves recognized that in the Hessian and Bavarian cities the start of construction is almost unfinanciable.
Since there are modern shuttle trains with motor and control vehicles at both ends, head stations no longer have problems with detouring and exiting.
Still: It starts with Stuttgart 21 including the new ICE track to Ulm.
This has been enforced by provincial and communal politicians.
Railways cooperate, also because the risks of the largest individual infrastructure project in the Czech Republic are borne by the taxpayer and not by the state concern.
Unrabbly, the project can still be excused in some way.
A new urban space will be created, which - if we believe the computer images of the designers - will contain a lot of green.
For the railway transport system, the S21 is detrimental.
The drive-through station and the construction of the line are intended for passenger transport, which - typically in the 80's - was attributed to a huge increase.
There was no competition for cheap flights.
However, modern transport policy must be primarily about strengthening freight transport, because rail transport is far more environmentally friendly than freight on the road.
But the new track is not suitable for freight trains due to steep climbs.
What is even more serious is that the S21 indirectly touches on many other railway projects.
