About the Person
Peter Frey was born on August 4, 1957 in Bingen.
He studied politics, pedagogy and romance.
He then worked for Südwestfunk and Frankfurter Rundschau.
In 1983, Frey switched to ZDF, first to "heute-journal".
Other stations: 1988 to 1990 personal officer editor-in-chief; 1991/92 correspondent in Washington; until 1998 lead and moderator "Morgenmagazins"; until 2001 head of foreign policy ZDF; since then head of ZDF's main studio.
After Brender, who was elected Medium Magazin Journalist of the Year on Wednesday, was deposed, the Greens announced they would file a constitutional lawsuit.
Koch's opposing candidate on the board, Rhine-Palatian Prime Minister Kurt Beck, explained last week that the Länder should change the state treaty with ZDF so that the intendant's personnel proposals could not be blocked by a politically motivated majority.
But Beck also declined the exclusion of party politicians from ZDF's supervisory boards.
Brender's presumed successor, Peter Frey, commanded the board with a clever chess move - immediately after Brender's deposal was heavily criticized by the committee.
Thanks to this evidence of journalistic independence, both Schächter was able to nominate him as editor-in-chief, so he could have been elected by the board of directors without suspecting that he had been pushed into office.
In party arithmetic, Frey is ranked among left-wing liberals.
The study gets rid of junk
Partial success for students: After a year of strikes, culture ministers finally went to the federal states on Thursday: At a conference of culture ministers and university rectors, both sides agreed that there was a will to improve study conditions for bachelors and magisters.
Students should be able to manage their studies in the future.
Specifically, they have agreed to reduce the load by the tests: In principle, each study module should be terminated by one single test.
In addition, the workload of learners should be checked, and a "real and defensible rate" should be guaranteed.
Politicians and universities want to simplify the recognition of exams between universities in Germany and abroad.
The federal states undertake to create a more flexible common structural assignment for bachelor's and master's fields of study.
Earth's deviations should cease to exist.
Fewer tests
Since the start of student protests in the worm, culture ministers and university rectors in their mouths have had winged words about better study.
For the first time, they are filled with specific content.
The so-called Accreditation Council decided that the number of examinations will be partially drastically reduced: "Each module will usually be terminated only by one exam," reads the 30-page report, which is intended in part to newly regulate the approval of fields of study.
"Significantly lower examination burdens," said Reinhold Grimm, chairman of the Accreditation Council, to be "halted the misdemeanous development, which significantly limited the difficulty of the study."
To do this, the "difficulty of study" in the future should be a "decisive criterion" in implementation.
Modules and contents "must be severely rechecked" to "unnecessarily restrict students when choosing
seminars."
Students would have "an entitlement to a space that allows and encourages their own initiative."
The rules should apply when approving new fields of study, but after review also for existing fields of study.
These need to be "re-accredited" every five years – but this means that it will last until 2015 before all fields of study are reviewed.
There are ten Accreditation Agencies responsible throughout Germany, which are subject to accritation boards established by the Länder.
In October, culture ministers urged "emphatically" Länder to act agencies in such a way that the content of the curriculum is not too large and the examination regime is not strict.
In individual Länder, Universities and universities have already agreed with the provincial ministries on appropriate measures.
As the only country to date, Rhineland-Palatinate has pledged additional resources for the further development of the Bologna process.
From the money, universities should primarily pay for consulting programmes and study counselling, and strengthen examination and foreign offices.
For this, countries and colleges want to spend ten million euros.
At the beginning of 2010, the government wants to submit an amendment to the parliament of a higher education law.
According to her, only one exam should be needed for the completion of the study module.
The formation of studies should be even more flexible, the recognition of examinations from other universities should be ensured, and the accessibility of the master should be facilitated.
By the end of next year, the complexity of all fields of study in the country should be checked.
The government and the opposition argued again on Thursday about study conditions in the country.
Criticism of the resolution has also fallen in addition to praise.
Juso, the Federal Board of Directors of higher education groups, has called for educational policy-making not to take place "behind closed doors" of ministers and university rectors in the future.
At the latest after the strike, "at least a word should speak into it" also students as persons concerned.
Future academics have also received support from the president of the Higher Rectors Conference (HRK), Margreta Wintermantelová: "We need the experience of students", she said on Thursday at one meeting with culture ministers, with whom HRK consulted about a joint reform report.
According to the Departments of Education and Science (GEW), Bonn resolutions didn't go far enough: They demand a legal claim that would allow every graduate with a bachelor's degree access to an extension master's degree.
Secondly, Länder must ensure that for today's generation of bachelor's students, there are enough places available tomorrow in master's fields of study with the best levels of study, GEW board member Andreas Keller said.
The federal states have to employ far more lecturers.
However, culture ministers have not commented on free access to master's programmes.
More than a footnote to Herta Müller
Getting to Germany and out is nice, almost no control..." is the opening line of one poem by the poet Werner Söllner, who in this torn-off language piece themates the experience with the arrival of one dissident who gained freedom, but at the same time lost his linguistic environment.
Werner Söllner was born in 1951 in Banat, Romania, first working as a teacher of German and English in Bucharest and later as a lecturer at a children's book publishing house, and 1982 moved to Frankfurt.
Söllner was honoured with various literary prizes, lecturing poetry at Frankfurt's legendary Goethe University in Frankfurt since 2020.
He took care of a considerable out-of-literary commotion last Tuesday at a seminar in Munich, which included "German literature in Romania in the mirror and the broken mirror of its security acts."
Söllner, who is clearly insecure and staunch, has left a statement in which he confesses his cooperation as a secret informant of the Romanian secret service Securitate in front of his fellow writers, including Richard Wagner, William Totok, Franz Hodjak and Helmuth Frauendorfer.
Söllner, together with some of those present, won the German Language Prize in 1989.
Attempts at recruitment began inconspicuously, at the earliest in 1971.
I'm, Söllner continued, "i.e. someone who hasn't resisted enough attempts at intimidation by Securitate."
Poet Werner Söllner is Secret Agent Walter.
Almost at the same time as the award of the Nobel Prize to Herta Müller and its unparalleled literary dealing with the mechanisms of the dictatorial government, the literary scene, and in particular the community of German authors coming from Romania, is shaken by a case that refers to the depths and disgrace of the political system, which is based on control and spying.
Werner Söllner is not, at least if it can be said according to the current state of knowledge, only the perpetrator, but he is also a victim.
In Securitate's acts, secret agent Walter played a role, Michael Markel said after Söllner's confession, referring to the positive influence of secret agent Walter.
He "cut out" me back then at all the essential points for him, Markel says.
He has a moral obligation to make that remark.
Söllner's statement was unexpected in this form and in front of this audience, but it is completely surprising the confession of the former dissident and his cooperation with the Romanian secret service.
Herta Müller wrote as early as July, when she read her own nudes, 914 pages, that Germany was a pleasant reservation for Securitate's spies.
Inquiry, it can be assumed, they will speculate and, in some cases, probably know which name is hidden behind the code name Sorin, Voicu and Walter.
Other than in the domestic, in the meantime, a very certain examination of Stazi components, the manipulation with the Securitate control estate is still very diffusal.
In recent years, systematic education has been obstructed and restricted.
One of the Birthler Offices, which corresponds to the National Council for the processing of Securitate (CNSAS), was founded only in 1999, the publication of the act has been rather lengthy since then.
Since 2005, there has been a law that governs the management of components.
The fact that former members of Securitate still have a great influence in Romania was documented after the publication of the Nobel Prize for Herta Müllerová, in which one of the former Securitate bosses of Timisoara expressed rejection about the winner of the award.
She says she's got psychosis, after all she's been interrogated only once.
The disgramment of power here has been expressed quite openly in the form of freedom of expression.
Of course, as in the context of concealed wrongdoing, there are unavoidable issues about the Söllner case.
Why only now? Who was harmed? The peculiar reaction of the listeners to Söllner’s confession is that the continuation of this chapter will be determined by the desire for knowledge rather than for guilt and shame.
It's more than a footnote to Herta Müller's work, which accepted the Nobel Prize yesterday.
