Heavyweight world championship fight
Passionate duel in Bern: Challenger Kevin Johnson constantly provoked the words and gestures of world champion Vitaly Klitschko.
However, after twelve hard rounds, the Ukrainian defended his heavyweight title of boxing organisation WBC.
The referee's statement was unanimous.
Humbark - Vitaly Klitschko remains the heavyweight boxing world champion.
The 38-year-old WBC champion beat American Kevin Johnson unanimously on points (120:108, 120:108, 119:109).
For Klitschko, it's the third successful defense of the title after his return in October 2008.
Previously, the Ukrainian was twice the world champion of WBO and WBC organizations.
Unfortunately, it didn't turn out the k.o. that everyone was expecting.
Klitschko improved his record to 39 wins in 41 professional fights and was defeated twice because of injuries.
Challenger Johnson was defeated for the first time in the 24th duel.
In front of about 17,000 spectators in the Bernese arena, the 2.02-meter-high Klitschko drove the duel primarily with his left.
The eleven-centimeter smaller Johnson, which announced the end of Klitschko's era before the duel, was withdrawing from the strokes of the world champion, so Klitschko couldn't strike the decisive blow with his dangerous right.
In total, Passive Johnson has done pretty little to challenge the challenger and was just trying to make it to the next rounds.
Klitschko provoked words and gestures, but his strokes rarely hit the world champion.
But even so, he's only the second boxer after Timo Hoffmann in 2000, who struggled with Klitschko's full twelve rounds.
The challenger took care of the commotion in the Swiss capital before the duel.
