During the weigh-in on Friday, the champion allowed himself to be provoked, withdrew Johnson's sunglasses from his nose "so he could see his eyes."
Fourth victory after Klitschko's return
Klitschko remained unbeaten even after his fourth fight after his return on October 11, 2008.
After a nearly four-year break, he defeated Nigerian Samuel Peter in Berlin and took back "his" WBC belt, which he had previously surrendered without a fight.
That year he defeated Juan Carlos Gomez of Cuba and Chris Arreol of the U.S. in September.
In this duel I received only one shot and physically I am in good shape, Klitschko reasoned a quick return to the ring.
His dream, however, remains the defense of all four important titles in the family.
Brother Vladimir is the world champion of WBO and IBF organizations.
The WBA belt is worn by Briton David Haye, after dethroning 2.13-metre-high Nikolai Vulayev in November.
Haye renounced a duel against Klitschko in the summer, which was scheduled for September.
His promoting company is reportedly planning a duel against Klitschko at London's Wembley Stadium.
Case Of Kunduz
A frontal attack from the opposition: Green faction chief Jürgen Trittin has called Guttenberg's defense minister a liar because of bad information about the Kunduz affair.
SPD and Linke (Left) call on the Chancellor to give an explanation.
Hamburg/Berlin - According to the latestest findings on the Kunduz airstrike, the opposition believes that Defense Minister Karl-Theodore zu Guttenberg (CSU) has committed a conscious deception.
The minister said "knowingly untruthfully" about the deadly bombing of the two tankers in September, the head of the Green faction Jürgen Trittin said on Saturday's news show ARD "Tagesschau."
Let's call it the usual expression: Lied.
An order to kill has been issued while disrespecting the rules of the International Security Assistance Force in Afghanistan (ISAF).
That's the essence of the report that NATO had, which Mr Guttenberg read, Trittin said.
On Friday, it became clear that the attack was not only about the destruction of the abducted trucks, but the targeted killing of two Taliban leaders.
In a U.S. airstrike demanded by Germany's Kunduz commander Colonel Klein, up to 142 people were killed or wounded, according to a NATO investigation.
The Greens, SPD and Linke urged Federal Chancellor Angela Merkel (CDU) on Saturday to comment immediately before the Bundestag and also clarify what Guttenberg really knew about the event.
Ms Merkel needs to explain whether the strategy of targeted killing is part of the Afghan policy of the Bundestag - and whether the Office of the Chancellor, the Bundeswehr and the intelligence service approved this new strategy were called for by Jürgen Trittin and Greens colleague Renate Künast.
Government spokesman Ulrich Wilhelm firmly dismissed the criticism.
The chancellor's office has no effect on the specific deployment of the Bundeswehr in Afghanistan, he said.
On Saturday, there were aloud doubts about the ministry's account of the background of the dismissal of Inspector General Wolfgang Schneiderhan and State Secretary Peter Wichert.
According to SPIEGEL's information, both Guttenberg were correctly and completely informed about the background of the attack on tankers.
It was officially stated that they were supposed to deny the existence of NATO reports and obscure the minister's sensitive details.
However, it was said from around the two top officials that they had alerted the Ministry of Defence even to several reports, among them a two-page document commanding Colonel Klein and one report by the military police.
"Frankfurter Allgemeiner Sonntagszeitung" reports that Wichert, meanwhile, should have asked in a letter to clarify the situation.
It is noted that he has not yet received any response.
The ministry did not comment on the complaints on Saturday, pointing out that this will be dealt with in the investigative committee of the Bundestag, which will be established next Wednesday.
Worlds of Dreams
Some see images in the waking state and hear voices that others cannot see and hear.
Not only mentally ill or in drug intoxication, the boundaries between imagination and reality merge.
As long as this happens in sleep, everything is fine.
If we lie with our eyes closed in our dark room, we are allowed to surround ourselves with the wildest notions and fanciful mirages.
However, those who experience dreaming states, see and hear things that others do not see and do not hear, in today’s Western world, are mostly worth a fool or drug-addicted, or at least as a weirdo.
However, such images and voices do not arise only in mentally ill patients or in drug intoxication.
Our imagination is to blame: The same neuronal processes that allow us to design houses, paint paintings, or write novels will make sure that the lines between dream, idea and reality merge here and there.
Dreams an altered state of consciousness can occur in all sorts of forms, at best we call them visions, in worse delusions.
While the initiator and the effect may vary, Hildegard von Bingen’s visions, the New-Age drug experiences of Carlos Castaneda, the shamanic states of delusion, psychosis and hallucinations can be derived from similar processes.
Hallucinations or visions arise when the balance of the brain's neuronal transmitters - primarily dopamine, norepinephrine, serotonin and acetylcholine - occurs, and the interaction between the different parts of the brain is disturbed.
Comprehensive hallucinations and vicious thoughts occur very often with schizophrenic psychosis.
Gravity doesn't have power over him
The connection between delusion and the dream employed philosophers and physicians since antiquity.
Aristotle understood hallucinations as a form of a dream in the waking state, Wilhelm Griesinger, one of the founders of modern psychology, noted in 1861 "the great similarity of delusion with dream states," for Sigmund Freud the dream was even "psychosis, with all nonsense, delusions, deception of the senses."
It's autumn 2001, Henning T. turns into a comic character.
His body is two-dimensional and garishly colored.
Gravity has no power over it, it floats at the ceiling of the room.
He feels kicked.
Behind him winged devilish figures with blood-red grimaces, valuing teeth.
He chases him, kicks him with heavy, fiery boots, tosses him against walls, feels pain on his whole body.
Loud, evil voices threaten from the walls and from the radio.
He can no longer endure the voices that scold and insult him.
Henning T. plucks two cables from the stereo, knots them in a loop and fastens it on a hook on the ceiling.
He stands on one loudspeaker and puts a loop around his neck.
He's jumping.
The loop permits.
Learnate yourself, shouting the choral voices tearing his drums, "go to the clinic and get treatment!"
Today Henningu T. is 40 years old, he experienced six acute psychoses, the last 2 years ago, the first almost 20 years ago.
LSD has been launched.
His diagnosis is schizoaffective paranoic psychosis, in addition to being manic-depressive, taking drugs against depression, mania, schizophrenia.
He's learned to live with his illness, he's married for three years.
In psychosis, I've experienced the worst fear and the deepest doubt, he says.
Wraiths are a terrible reality
About one percent of people get schizophrenia at least once in their lifetime, in psychiatric clinics it is the second most commonly treated disease after depression.
In acute schizophrenic psychosis, disabilities are usually no longer able to distinguish the internal and external world, getting into an overable state that can lead to hallucinations, magnitude, relationship illusions or the illusion of persecution.
Particularly widespread is the hearing of voices - votes that command, comment or conduct dialogues.
Optical hallucinations are not uncommon either.
The afflicted is unable to distinguish the important from the unimportant, the brain cannot filter out distracting signals, consciousness is flooded with impressions.
Just as a dream opens psychosis of a flood of thoughts and fantasies that come from the deepest layers of consciousness.
The perception of reality in psychosis is centered on the subject, says Professor Thomas Fuchs, chief medical officer of the Department of General Psychiatry of the University Clinic in Heidelberg.
Schizophrenic is located in the center of events, everything has a meaning directed at him.
At the same time, he gets into a passive role, becomes, like in a dream, overwhelmed by events and cannot actively shape them.
Schizophrenics, therefore, often feel threatened, controlled and manipulated.
Comparison with reality is usually not possible for them, ghosts are a terrible fact, awakening is excluded.
Once in a life to experience hallucination
Other than in a dream, Fuchss says, "but the sensory and spatial structure of reality that a person perceives in psychosis is fundamentally preserved."
Delusional ideas and hallucinations are based on reality, the alienation of the experience occurs in the real framework.
Scientists are always in front of the riddle, why exactly our brain is doing flips like this.
Exceptional conditions can lead each person to lose their relationship to reality in the short term, Fuchs says.
However, not everyone develops into psychosis.
There appears to be a genetic disposition to schizophrenia, but studies on twies have shown that genes are not the only determining factor.
The researchers assume that affected people have minimal brain damage.
We believe that at an early stage of development there will be damage to the neuronal maturation of the brain, for example, the mother's viral disease during pregnancy, Fuchs explains.
But it remains a riddle of how the neuronal system malfunction, which has not manifested itself for many years.
Influences of the environment also play a role - stress or traumatic experiences can be an initiator of psychosis if there is disposition.
Also hallucinogenic drugs can trigger psychosis because they interfere with the transmission system.
When our brain boils
What's crazy is that psychotronics suffer from their hallucinations, and to get rid of them, they swallow expensive medications, others give money for drugs to get them, says psychology professor Erich Kasten from Lübeck.
Whether we perceive mirages as pleasant or unpleasant is often just a matter of attitude.
Kasten has been collecting reports on hallucinations and mirages for a decade. Last year he interpreted his results in "Die irreale Welt in unserem Kopf".
Hallucinations belong to the typical accompanying phenomena of psychosis, but also to surprisingly many other diseases.
In addition to psychological disorders such as borderline personality or depression, diseases such as migraine, epilepsy, multiple sclerosis or Alzheimer’s or brain trauma are often.
Surprisingly, it hallucinates under certain conditions even the brain of a healthy person.
Hallucinations, according to Kasten, consist of the contents of ideas that are normally suppressed.
They arise when the parts of the brain in which sensory perception is processed are too active or under-suppressed; if our brain boils, or when there is a lack of stimulation.
Drugs can trigger this condition as well as brain damage or psychosis.
But also the withdrawal of stimuli, stress, worries with love or other other states of emergency.
In more recent studies, up to one-third of respondents reported having experienced hallucinations at least once in their lives.
In the summer of 2005, Sarah K. is 16 years old, living in a small town in Mecklenburg-Vorpommern and going to the local grammar school.
In fact, a perfectly normal girl.
If only there weren't those shapes and voices.
During breaks, it may happen that she is approached by a cycling with long, dark hair.
When she responds to him, classmates look at her with a surprise - no one but her sees and hears this boy.
This timid girl considers an uptight dreamer, but Sarah K. doesn't dream, she knows.
He's awake, he hears and sees the boy, just like he hears and sees a girl sitting next to each other in a bench.
He can even feel his presence, at least for a few moments.
It's a pleasant feeling, she likes the boy.
Am I crazy?, he sometimes asks.
But he knows that this boy and other mirages aren't real and disappear again.
At night, appearances are rather daunting
Often, mirages are loads.
When he's sitting in the classroom, he can't keep track of the lessons many times, because the teacher in her eyes and ears just introduces a new classmate, but he explains to everyone else audibly and visibly on the board of the mathematical equation.
At night, appearances are rather daunting.
When she waits in her dark room for a sleep that rarely arrives and too late, she can often not distinguish whether she is awake or if she's already asleep, whether the paintings she sees, grimaces, or a girl with bleeding cut wounds on her hands and feet arise in front of her open or closed eyes.
Leave me alone, they're yelling at those characters.
It doesn't mean anything to parents and friends about it, they'd think she's gone mad.
Autumn 2009. Sarah K. is 20 years old today.
She graduated last year and lives with her boyfriend.
Hallucinations stopped a year ago.
Ever since she contacted psychologist Kasten online, she knows exactly that she hasn't gone mad.
Delusions are also an art out of desperation
If someone is socially isolated or stimuluses are taken away from him, he will very likely have hallucinations, Kasten says.
I don't think the brain has enough jobs and takes care of its own fun.
Sarah K., an only child, was afraid of the other children as a little girl, often playing for herself.
Her hallucinations began when she learned at 13 that the man she had been calling her dad since she was thinking is not her own father.
Because visual and acoustic hallucinations are active in the same brain regions, which also process real perceptions and sounds, it is difficult for those affected as Sarah K. to distinguish between delusions and reality. "Every experience," says Kasten, "takes place in the brain, also reality is displayed there.
Hallucinations are also images that let the brain develop, that's which is why it acts as real."
Also nocturnal dreams evaluate Kasten as a form of hallucination.
Hallucination of healthy people does not differ fundamentally from hallucination in acute psychosis.
Compared to schizophrenics, mentally healthy people are able to identify their delusions as unrealistic.
Therefore, Kasten advises its patients to evaluate hallucinations as an extraordinary ability.
Border state between dream and hallucination
In our world focused too much, the imaginary experience is unfortunately mostly pathologized, says Michael Schmidt-Degenhard, a professor at Heidelberg and chief medical officer of Florence-Nightingale Hospital in Düsseldorf.
Psychosis is a severe, painful disease, but it also brings positives, creativity - mirage is also an art out of despair.
Schmidt-Degenhard explored the so-called oneiroidal form of experiences, a special form of altered consciousness towards a dream.
Oneiroid states are highly complex, detailed fictitious dreams, which the affected person considers to be real and can not even retroactively distinguish them from the waking state.
Oneiroid state of consciousness arises in extreme situations caused by trauma or diseases, for example, patients with polyradicular inflammation, who experience motor paralysis in full consciousness.
When a person is threatened by the loss of the world and of himself, perhaps by his continuing, complete inability to move, he replaces life-threatening real situations with his own, imaginary world that is disconnected from reality, says Schmidt-Degenhard.
A type of boundary state between a dream and hallucination.
Since it is not possible to manage a situation in reality, there will be a change of worlds, but the real situation will become present.
An intense form of supporting the senses and the transfer of the senses into some unbearable situation, which allows us people their own ability to form in an instant into the loss of consciousness.
Everything is real, even later in memory
Also art historian Peter Cornelius Claussen describes his oneiroid experiences in his book "Herzwechsel" (Heart exchange) as a soul rescue effort.
He was 49 years old when, after heart valve surgery with a subsequent heart transplant, his consciousness set off on a several-day journey, with his body lying motionless in the intensive care unit.
Claussen plunged into the world he had created, visited alien times and places: He became an old man bedridden, in a train sanatorium, a robot guarding his health.
He lives in the early Middle Ages among the nobility, which produces narcotic substances from blood.
On a motorbike he is hijacked by a Greek to his homeland, on a hospital bed he surfs at a company in the Alps and meets a Korean mobster.
It delves completely into the imaginary action, everything is real, even later in memories.
These internal paths are fundamentally different for Claussen from any dream.
A dream is a different state, he describes his experience.
In contrast to dreams, the memories from those mental journeys are still clearly clear after years and insignificant details.
Even clearer and more intense than a real experience.
The intensity of this experience breaks down the horizon of our other true experiences, says psychiatrist Schmidt-Degenhard.
After all, they show oneiroid conditions, psychosis, hallucinations, and drug intoxication, how fragile our internal images of external reality are even in the waking state.
Any change in the state of consciousness is summarized by psychologist Kasten, "may lead to the deformation of what we call reality."
A reality that can also be rewarding.
