Promise of FonPeek
Peek is extremely limited, used only to view mail, without the ability to view a certain type of attachments or surf the network.
He enjoyed his five-minute fame in the U.S. when Time magazine recognized him as one of the products of the year.
It's harder to see him outside, but the idea can be interesting, especially for young people.
Unlike smartphones, Peek is characterized by a very low price and monthly payments, less than half the amount that is paid, for example, for an iPhone data plan.
After its success, the company began to offer two new devices: one is intended exclusively for Twitter, the other on the basis of a permanently provided free data service, but for which it is necessary to pay a six-fold amount for Peek.
FonPeek, which was unveiled at the Le Web event yesterday, is a European version of the invention and at the same time its third transformation.
It is limited just like its American counterpart, but it brings surprises.
The monthly price for maintenance is about 12 euros per month and includes roaming throughout Europe.
People traveling with a smartphone already know what it means to lose data services after crossing the border or impatiently looking for a cafe with WiFi (or Fon connection) in order to view the mail.
The ideal with rooming at a fixed price is incredibly attractive and should have been introduced a long time ago.
Yes, some operators have special contracts and travel plans, but in my experience, one would need a doctorate in physics to understand them, and also very good glasses to be able to read miniature letters.
The European Commission is constantly demanding that roaming tariffs be reduced so that they are accessible to the whole of Europe, which still does not have the desired effect.
There is a reasonable concern about the use of data services outside the home state.
The fact that such a device as FonPeek (it is interesting that it continues to have the name Fon when it does not have WiFi) achieves this type of tariff, means that operators begin to think differently and consider different offers to compensate for their data network.
Kindle is another nice example, offering book downloads and other searches on the Internet.
No contracts, no complications.
This device is connected and is paid when purchased, not after months.
And that’s exactly what I expect to happen someday in caseFonPeek, it’s an option to buy with permanent access like Peek has already introduced in the U.S.
It'sn't because I want to buy it.
I'm looking at mail on the phone, thank you.
I'm expecting it because I think it's the best type from a consumer's point of view.
It would be interesting if the same system could be used on a computer.
The color that Michelle adores
Yellow is undoubtedly the favorite color of the first lady of the USA.
For Barack Obama's appointment as U.S. president, she chose a dress and coat in this color, choosing the same color when she accompanied him at the second most important moment of his life: the awarding of the Nobel Peace Prize in Oslo.
Michelle Obama used very similar models in both cases: in addition to the fact that it is a similar color - yellow-green with glittering effects - in both cases it was a three-piece set - a dress, a bag and a coat made of the same fabric - adorned with a large embroidery or collar.
At first glance, the first lady of the U.S. decided to follow in the footsteps of an Asturian princess and wear similar clothes.
The fabric from which the clothes are made makes both models considerably different.
On the occasion of taking office, Michelle used large lace, in Oslo velvet with print.
It also changes accessories: green shoes with a medium-high heel, now a similar model, but in gold color.
He also wears various hairstyles: this time, this time, previously dissolved hair.
75% of working mothers have maternity-related work problems.
Discrimination at work has increased in recent years, especially among women of younger years who intend to have a child.
This is the result of the study 'Fertility and Working Career of Spanish Women', conducted by CSIC in collaboration with the Women's Institute.
The study involved 9,737 women between the ages of 15 and 75, and the results are unambiguous: 75% of working mothers have limited opportunities in the labour market because of maternity.
The study emphasizes that at the beginning of cohabitation, there are many differences of opinion on the number of children, depending on whether they work or not, and also according to the type of employment.
Women who don't work away from home, work part-time or don't have a stable job, start living with their partner earlier, giving birth to their first child and overall having more children.
On the other hand, women with stable employment, especially in the public sector, put off coexistence and motherhood, and have fewer offspring.
"Women from 35 to 49 years of age with stable employment have an average of 3.7 years from the beginning of cohabitation with a partner. This figure is 4.1 in the case of women who have devoted a greater part of their lives to achieving their stable employment and have therefore had to postpone the cohabitation and arrival of children.
The type of profession also has a strong influence on maternity, albeit a minor influence on the period of beginning coexistence with a partner,” explains Margarita Delgado, who is engaged in research at CSIC.
Another factor affecting the age of women at the time of marriage the birth of the offspring is the degree of education.
The research shows that the higher the level of education, the higher the age when women get married and have their first child.
There are also differences between women of the same generation.
For example, in the 35 to 49-year-old age, women with primary and higher education gave birth to their first child at the age of 25.1 or 32.1.
"There is yet another difference between women depending on their level of education.
Women with basic education get married and give birth to their first child even before they find a stable job, while women with secondary or high education proceed in the opposite way prefer to get a stable job, before marriage and the birth of their first child,” Delgado said.
