How much should you collect for your Facebook profile?
Facebook users can familiarize themselves with the salary they deserve for their profile, thanks to the new Trovit app, the search engine with the largest number of sorted advertisements from real estate, employment and motor vehicles in Spain.
How much should you take? calculates users’ salaries on the basis of more than 140,000 job offers available to Trovit in Spain, using a form that is available on Facebook, where you need to insert a profession, years of experience, age and the city where the candidate wishes to work.
During the week the app works, the search engine found that the Spanish worker generally "feels poorly rewarded," Albert Rivera, responsible for Trovit's product, explained in his report.
According to data available to Trovit from its search engines in the UK and France, Spaniards are the hardest-to-pay.
The British programmer takes 45,226 euros gross annually and France's 31,059, while the Spaniard receives 24,000.
Likewise, the driver has a salary of 34,247 euros net per year in England, 25.751 in France and 16.420 in Spain.
According to Trovit, in Spain the worst-paid work of a waiter with 11.592 euros is net per year, followed by a salesman with 14,725 euros and a worker with 15,667 euros.
The most wanted jobs on the network in the last three months are the driver, part-time employment and social worker.
Bizlaz set date for Zelay's departure
The Brazilian government has set a deadline for Manuel Zelaya to remain at its embassy in Tegucigalpa.
The deposed president must leave the diplomatic mission no later than January 27, 2010, when his mandate officially expires.
Francisco Catunda, chargé d'affaires of the Brazilian embassy in Hondura's capital, confirmed to TV Globo that "Mel" knows he must leave the embassy by that date, where he has been hiding since September 21.
"He is aware that on January 27, after the end of his mandate, he must leave and seek another refuge."
The station also quoted a former statesman who confirmed his intention to leave the embassy before the deadline: "I intend to leave as soon as possible, logically under the auspices of the Brazilian government," Zelaya said by telephone.
After the November 29 election (when Porfirio Lobo was elected), Zelaya assured that he would remain at the Brazilian embassy until January.
However, the same week he tried to take refuge in Brazil.
The operation eventually failed because Zelaya rejected the condition of political asylum sought by his "de facto" government, chaired by Roberto Micheletti.
Thank you
After days of wine and roses since last spring, when asset inflation represented the prevailing mood in the markets, there has been uncertainty in the markets again, which should not be surprising, because it is already a natural state in an environment where executives take decisions about the future, which is itself uncertain.
The drug used by central banks to stabilise the market was the monetization of debt by directly purchasing assets and the indirect loosening of liquidity at a near-zero course so that financial institutions could buy assets for deduction.
Central banks have already confirmed that direct asset purchases have already practically ended, and without questioning their disparate liquidity policies, are starting to throw a little bit of sand into gears, as the Spanish Central Bank did with its auction last year.
Therefore, investors doubt the ability of the world economy to maintain an economic recovery without such heterodox measures, and we participate in the return of the reduction of risk returns.
Stock exchange mobility and corporate bond differences have regained points, although they are still very distant from the abnormal level they achieved at the beginning of the year.
States were forced to socialize the private risk, which once again affected public debt.
Once again pushing for quality, the U.S., Japan and Germany have taken refuge with elected investors, thus expanding the differentiation of eurozone countries towards Germany.
Spain was almost not affected by this instability, but Standard & Poor's arrived at that time and threatened a new revision to reduce the qualifications of the Kingdom of Spain.
On the other hand, some uncertainty is not useless for silencing moral risk defenders who have once again struggled to burden the consciences of those responsible for world economic policy in order to hinder the destructive consequences of limiting credit to employment, when, in fact, a new Thanksgiving day would have to be declared.
Nevertheless, the situation in Dubai represents tribal struggles between emirs for the restoration of power in the emirates, and as for Greece, we have seen this before.
A government that obscures the deficit, elections, and a new government is stepping in.
Prodi did it in Italy after Berlusconi and Sócrates in Portugal after Durão Barroso.
It makes no sense for Ecofin to save Hungary from non-compliance with foreign payments at the beginning of the year and to allow the euro's member country not to meet its obligations.
But what makes sense is for Ecofin to force the Greek government into transparency and obligation of its own citizens to take common accountability with the need for extraordinary measures to maintain the stability of its public finances.
If, as we expect, we find ourselves before the period of normalisation of premiums from a temporary risk, after some excesses in supported positions would be within expectations.
In Spain's case, while our differences in public debt will increase as well as the bonds of European companies, we don't have to worry.
If European disparities decrease and ours continue to expand, this would be a typical Spanish risk.
Standard & Poor's is just an anecdote. When our leading indicators force most of the studio's services to anticipate their recovery scenarios and revise their 2011 rise scenario, including OCDE and the European Commission to return to the Japanese scenario, for the Spanish economy, this doesn't have much hope of living to meet this warning.
Again, it looks like it will be a great opportunity to buy Spanish assets, albeit for the time being "carefully, my friend drivers".
