President Iberdrola Ignacio Sánchez Galán was the first to intervene yesterday before the subcommittee of the Chamber of Deputies, where Spain's energy strategy will be discussed in those days over the next two decades.
After his intervention, followed by a speech by President Gas Natural-Fenos, Rafael Villaseca, Galán gave the press a few remarks about the proposal, which he submitted to the subcommittee behind closed doors.
According to President Iberdrol, Spain needs an investment of 50,000 million euros by 2020 and another 60,000 million from that date by 2030 if nuclear power plants that are currently in operation are retained.
If this did not happen, the necessary investment will not be 60,000 million, but up to 95,000 million.
Indeed, Iberdrola believes that over the next decade, investments must focus not on increasing production capacity, but on distribution networks and international interconnections
From 2020 onwards, the issue is changing and performance will need to be increased.
Its offer focuses on gas plants and coal-fired power plants with mechanisms for capturing carbon dioxide and, above all, on renewable energies.
In this case, it considers that a new 18,000 MW is needed, but with other "supportive energies."
Galán, who believes that a draft law on a sustainable economy that has just been approved by the government, corresponds to the line demanded by the enterprise, he did not want to leave like a royal decree supporting the consumption of national coal, which contradicts the policy of combating climate change.
In his view, there is a "clear long-term bet" in this sense, despite "exact" measures, as President Iberdrola called them.
Whatever the case, Galán believes that investment decisions, although long-term, must be taken now, because their maturation lasts and a stable tax framework is needed to launch them.
Ferrovial wins 'handling' Aer Lingus at ten airports
Ferrovial's subsidiary for airport land services, the Swiss company Swissport, will play the role of a preferential partner of Irish airline Aer Lingus for cost services at ten European airports where it is present.
