Acciona is aiming for its first highway in Australia against Hochtief and Bouygues
Acciona is fighting for its first motorway concession in Australia, a market where it is already working as an energy and water management firm.
The construction, which is concerned, is a tunnel estimated at 1.700 million Australian dollars (about 1.047 million euros).
Its rivals are the French company Bouygues and Germany's Hochtief.
A tunnel 5 kilometres long in the Australian city of Brisbane on the east coast has become the closest option for Acciona to establish itself in this country as a construction and concession highway business.
The town hall wishes to have the tunnel completed at the end of 2014
After the selection of public-private cooperation (PPP according to initials in English) for the further development of the project in a business plan backed by Erns & Young, the administration already has a final list of candidates for the implementation of construction and the operation of tolls.
Bids are expected to be submitted in the month of May, and the projected investment will reach 1.700 million Australian dollars (1.047 million euros) and, in the last phase of competitions, three groups with a significant presence in European buildings.
The Transcity group is led by Acciona, the only Spanish company in the competition, and its companions are BMD and Ghella.
The first is one of Australia's largest construction companies, and the second based in Italy is one of Europe's largest underground construction specialists.
Northern Read is at play against Acciona with the presence of British Laing O'Rourke, Australian infrastructure manager Transfield and French construction group Bouygues.
The third and last group, which passed through the sieve, has the name LBRJV and involves the Australian capital Leighton (a subsidiary of the construction German company Hochtief, represented by ACS), the local Baulderstone (ownership of the German group Bilfinger Berger) and an important company specializing in civilian buildings Razel.
Australia's infrastructure market promises large-scale construction and privatizations, but entails a clear difficulty of entry in a way other than through the acquisition of a local construction firm.
In addition to Asian references, the strategy being followed by Hochtief or Bilfinger is still an example for Spanish giants in the sector.
ACS has acquired a position on the market through its 30% stake in Hochtief, the owner quoted by Leighton.
Million-dollar assets
Acciona is betting on a geographical area with the greatest importance for a given sector, where it already has a significant presence through its energy supplier activity and in desalination trade.
Among other assets, it has the largest wind park in Waubra (192 MW) in Victoria and leads a group of businesses that build and manage desalination facilities in Adelaide with a budget of £££££££££700
Its plan is now to consolidate these two areas and to use common energy to enter the areas of construction and the operation of infrastructures.
An underground road subject to a toll, promoted by Brisbane’s Bureau of Infrastructure, called the Northern Link (North Link), is supposed to connect the western highway from Toowong with a circular route in Kelvin Grove (see map) in the length of a 5-kilometre tunnel. It is the largest event to reduce Brisbane’s traffic congestion.
The calendar set by the local administration plans the assignment for the summer of 2010 and the start of works for December next year.
Although this paid transit would open in the last months of 2014, full completion of the works is not planned until 2016.
The tunnel began to be designed in 2005.
It will contain two currents in each direction, an electronic toll system and a comprehensive ventilation system.
Queensland's government will provide 500 million Australian dollars (308 million euros) for its funding.
Brisbane has already offered this contract to Hochtief
Australia still brings Hochtief a significant profit as it yesterday announced a contract with the government estimated at 154 million.
The ACS will operate through its Leighton local branch infrastructure of the wide-wide bandwidth with optical fiber cable.
This situation will allow 400,000 people quick access to the internet and will provide the work of a German company for 18 months.
On the other hand, the company has abandoned the 27-storey King George construction project in Brisbane, Australia. Construction work was yet to begin, and Hochtief closed the sale for 129 million.
In the UK, specifically in Manchester, he will participate with Laing O'Rourke in the construction and operation of two school centres for 25 years.
The initial investment will be 75 million euros.
The FCC was allocated a tunnel in Slovenia for 64 million
FCC, through its subsidiary Alpine, has been awarded the construction of a 2.1-kilometre tunnel for a highway in Slovenia worth 64.5 million euros.
The company won the contract a few days after carrying out works to expand the tunnel in Bosruck (Austria) for 130 million.
