Fine of £63million for France Télécom for hampering competition
The regulatory authority for competition in France imposed a fine of 63 million euros lFrance Télécom for anti-competitive behaviour in caribbean areas belonging to the country.
According to this institution, the operator has limited the competition of fixed and mobile phones by signing exclusive agreements, pricing plans and loyalty programs with consumers on the margins of regulation.
With this sanction, France's former monopoly and still state-owned company have imposed more than 560 million euros of fines since 1994 to impose obstacles to their competitors.
The fine imposed yesterday was de facto increased because the authority overseeing the competition believes that France Télécom has committed a similar offense as in the past and that there has therefore been a relapse.
App store
