Playing in the Champions league has yielded 476.4 million to Madrid and Barçe since the beginning
The Champions League has become a source of income for these clubs since its inception in 1992.
To the extent that Real Madrid and Barcelona have earned 476.4 million since then, making them the third and fifth-highest-earning club in the competition.
The charter is led by Manchester United.
The charter is led by Manchester United.
Winning the UEFA Champions League doesn't just bring glory. It's also one of the main sources of income for big clubs.
Only to the extent that the competition divided 5.362.5 million euros among 105 teams (of which 12 were Spanish), which participated in this event since the creation of the tournament in the 1992/1993 season, according to UEFA data.
Real Madrid and F. C. Barcelona came out very well from this division.
The White Club was third on the continent with the most money it earned for participating, and the only one along with AC Milan, who won the grand prize three times.
Catala's current champist and the last winner of Europe's defunct Cup in 1992 is fifth in the classification.
The list is led by Manchester United and Bayern in Munich, the two teams that also charged the most money to participate in last year's Champions League.
And that's even though she's both lost to Barça.
Spain, on the other hand, is only the third country to receive the most money through the Champions, despite winning the most titles of all (five against four for Italy, three for England and two for Germany).
Distribution
This apparent paradox is explained by the complex process in distributing money used by UEFA.
For this financial year, an authority chaired by Michel Platini is planning to make 1.090 million for the Champions League.
Out of this money, UEFA devotes 413.1 million to fixed payments: each of the 20 clubs that took part in the preliminary classification round (among others Atlético Madrid) received £2.1 million.
In addition, those who achieved participation received 3.8 million only for their presence, in addition to another 550,000 euros for each match in which they participate.
There is also a premium for winning (800,000 euros) and for a draw (400,000 for each squad). Eight-finalists get three million each; eight quarter-finalists3.3 million.
Each of the last four will get 4 million.
The champion earns 9 million, while the second-ranked finalist makes £5.2million.
In this way, the club earns a minimum of 7.1 million and a maximum of 31.2 million.
Barça, on the other hand, won 2.5 million for winning the European Super Cup in August.
In addition, however, there are variable payments, for which 337.8 million are intended.
This money is divided according to the relative value of each television market.
Spanish is one of the mains and has four places.
Last year's champion will get 40% of the allocated money, the second 30%, the third20% and the fourth 10%.
The second part will be paid according to the number of matches that will take place this year.
Nueva Rumasa offers 61 million euros for 29.9% Sos
Nueva Rumasa's group yesterday released the details of an offer to buy shares, with the help of which it wants to get the top spot between 25% to 29.9% of Sos capital.
This percentage does not obligate to make a public offer for the acquisition (option) of all securities.
